# -*- mode: sh; sh-shell: bash -*-
# vim: set ft=bash:
# shellcheck shell=bash
# shellcheck disable=SC2016  # the rule dependencies are single quoted so that they survive verbatim
# shellcheck disable=SC2153  # 'RAD_BIN' and the record separators are declared in Bar.d/radicle_lib

### The radicle domain of bar: what this checkout is, what it carries, whether the author of an
### incoming item may be trusted, how a topic starts -- and how a repository gets onto radicle in
### the first place.
###
### Six helpers, all served by the backing library Bar.d/radicle_lib (running 'rad', reading its
### padded tables back, the age of a last event, the node storage, the '.worktrees/' exclusion and
### the JSON literals):
###
###   bar rad_state [--json] [--max <n>]                     the whole state, one screen
###   bar rad_patches [--state <s>] [--author <a>] [--json] [--max <n>]
###                                                          only the patches, filtered
###   bar rad_trust --repo <rid> --author <who>              is that author a trusted source
###   bar rad_comment_metadata <item> --query <label|assign|unassign>
###                                                          the trusted '*label:'/'*assign:'/
###                                                          '*unassign:' proposals of one item
###   bar rad_topic <class> "<description>"                  open the topic tree of an item
###   bar rad_import                                         import this repository into radicle
###
### Every helper answers '-h' or '--help' with its usage on stdout and exit 0.
###
### 'rad_state' answers the questions an agent asks before it touches a radicle repository: which
### repository is this (RID, name, path), which identity does 'rad' use here, how the patches stand
### (how many, by state, ours against others), how the issues stand (state, labels, when something
### happened last) and which local branches still carry commits no patch exposes.  'rad_patches' is
### the patch section of that report, filtered by state and by author.
###
### Nothing there goes to the network: 'rad patch/issue list' answer from the local node storage and
### the divergence is measured against 'rad/main' as the last 'git fetch rad' left it, which the
### 'head' line names.  Syncing is the caller's business ('rad sync', 'git fetch rad'): this helper
### reports what is there and says how old it is.  'rad_topic' is the one exception -- it fetches the
### radicle remote (else 'origin') to *know* how the base relates to upstream, see below.
###
### SOURCES
###
###   rad inspect --rid         the repository identity, 'git rev-parse' the toplevel
###   rad self                  alias and DID of the identity 'rad' uses here
###   rad patch list --all      patch id, title, author and head (the state is not in the list)
###   rad issue list --all      issue id, title, author, labels and assignees
###   rad patch/issue list --authored/--draft/--merged/--archived/--closed/--solved
###                             rad prints one state per call, so the states are subtracted as
###                             sets: 'open' is what is in '--all' and in none of the others, and
###                             'ours' is what '--authored' (patches) or '(you)' (issues) says
###   the node storage          time and kind of the last event of every patch and issue, read
###                             from 'refs/namespaces/*/refs/cobs/xyz.radicle.<type>' below
###                             '$(rad path)/storage/<RID>' with 'git for-each-ref'
###   git for-each-ref/rev-list the current branch, the local branches and their distance from
###                             'refs/remotes/rad/main' or 'refs/remotes/rad/master'
###   git ls-remote/log/branch  'rad_topic': the trunk on both sides and the commits in between
###
### OUTPUT
###
###   Text: one header line per section -- '<section>  <counts>' -- then one line per item, the
###   free text last so that nothing has to be padded.  Ages are short ('6m', '2h', '1y') and are
###   those of the last event.  '--json': one object marked with 'bar_rad': 1, the complete data,
###   never elided.
###   At most '--max' items per section (default 10, '--max 0' lists all).  A section which is
###   cut states exactly how much ('n of N elided'), and when anything was cut the complete text
###   is written to a file whose path the last line prints, so the elision loses nothing.
###
###   'ours' is what 'rad' itself calls ours, everything else is 'other' (whose author is then
###   named behind the title); a patch which rad lists in more than one state is named by the
###   first of 'draft', 'merged', 'archived'.
###
### EXIT CODES
###
###   0  answered (an empty repository -- no patch, no issue -- is an answer, not a failure)
###   2  usage error
###   3  no data: not a git repository, or no radicle repository here
###   1  could not answer: 'rad' is missing or failed, or its output was not understood
###
### 'rad_trust' answers whether the author of an incoming issue or patch counts as a *trusted
### source* for the triage policy (`incoming-issue-triage` §3): a delegate of that repository, or an
### identity with at least <threshold> authored commits.  The numbers come from the fact tool
### 'tools/radicle_triage/triage-facts' (delegates from the identity document, the commits an
### identity authored as its own NID from the COB commits, plus the code commits of the humans
### behind its patches); this helper is the one action which turns them into the verdict and the exit
### code, so the question costs no agent turn and no prose:
###
###   bar rad_trust --repo rad:z3WhBdHt… --author cehteh
###   trusted author=cehteh delegate=yes authored=396 threshold=10 reason=delegate of this repository repo=rad:z3WhBdHt1VVNyeQ61zpY66pNzuSrP
###
###   exit 0 trusted -- 1 untrusted -- 2 could not answer (unknown author, no data, usage)
###
### 'rad_comment_metadata' is the second half of the same policy, for the writes nobody but a
### delegate may make.  Radicle authorizes an issue's `Label`, `Assign` and `Lifecycle` actions to
### the repository delegates (and to the issue's author for editing and closing it) -- verified in
### the implementation (`heartwood`, 'crates/radicle/src/cob/issue.rs', 'Issue::authorization'), so
### an incoming contributor *cannot* label or assign anything: the store refuses it.  Instead the
### proposal travels as a **structured comment**: the first lines of a comment may be
###
###   *label:improvement
###   *assign:cehteh
###   *unassign:ct_vibes
###
### one per line, starting at the comment (the issue description counts -- rad calls it the root
### comment of the thread).  This helper reads those blocks back from the node storage and returns
### only the entries a trusted source wrote:
###
###   bar rad_comment_metadata <item> [--query <key>].. [--repo <rid|path>] [--trust <spec>].. [--last] [--json]
###
### The form is '*<key>:<value>' and the vocabulary is open: a key is a word of letters, digits, '_'
### and '-' which starts with a letter, and *any* key is read back -- what a key means is the
### caller's business, and only the keys with a registered effect ('label', 'assign', 'unassign')
### are ever applied (see the write layer below).  The block ends at the first line which is not a
### marker, so a marker quoted inside a reply is prose, and a value is a single word -- anything else
### is dropped, never handed back half cut.  '--query' names the keys to answer for and repeats;
### with no '--query' at all the answer carries every key a comment proposes, and every line names
### its own key.
###
### '<item>' is the issue or patch the question is about, short or full -- one item only, not the
### whole repository: the ref listing is a single call, the chains (the ops and their payloads) are
### opened for that COB alone.  An id the node does not hold is 'no data' (3), an id an issue and a
### patch both match is a usage error (2).
###
### '--trust' takes 'did:<did|nid>' (that identity, named) and 'contributor(<n>)' (an identity with
### at least <n> authored commits); with no '--trust' only the **delegates** are trusted, which is
### the narrow default.  '--last' keeps the newest block *per key* -- the last record of the chain
### order, not of the hash order: two markers written in the same second are ordered by where they
### sit in the chain (parent before child), which is what makes 'the newest block' mean what it says.
### A block which is the newest word on two keys answers for both, and a newer block nobody can be
### attributed to is skipped, so an older trusted block still applies.
### The trust facts come from the same tool 'rad_trust' uses ('tools/radicle_triage/triage-facts').
### A delegate's maintenance command reads the trusted proposals back and applies them -- the labels
### and assignments themselves still need that delegate's own key.
###
### The write layer of the same policy.  One function per effect, each of them exactly one 'rad'
### invocation, and none of them asks about trust: that is the reader's decision, which is why the
### reader is the only way in.  These are not rules -- they are the API a rule (or the daemon the
### project builds elsewhere) composes, which is why they are named like the plumbing they are:
###
###   bar rad_set_label <item> <add> [<delete>]..      one 'rad issue|patch label' write
###   bar rad_assign <item> <did|alias>                one 'rad issue|patch assign' add
###   bar rad_unassign <item> <did|alias>              one 'rad issue|patch assign' delete
###   bar rad_apply_from_comments <item>.. [--query <key>].. [--repo <rid|path>] [--trust <spec>]..
###                              [--dry-run]           the trusted proposals of an item, applied
###
### '<item>' is the form the reader answers with ('issue:<id>', 'patch:<id>'); a bare id is an issue.
### The identity of an assignment is resolved before it is written -- a comment cannot be trusted to
### spell a DID right -- by the same fact command the trust gate asks, and a value which resolves to
### nothing is refused rather than written as it stands.  Every applier reports what it applied
### ('applied label item=issue:4444444 add=idea delete=wip') and no applier writes into the thread.
###
### 'rad_apply_from_comments' reads the newest trusted block per key (that is what applies; a block a
### later one superseded is history) and dispatches every entry to the applier of its key: 'label',
### 'assign', 'unassign'.  Any other key is reported and never guessed at.  An entry whose author
### cannot be attributed never reaches it -- the reader drops those, silently, like every proposal
### which is not trusted.  A label the item already says is not written again ('skip when the labels
### already say it'), the previous chain state is deleted in the same write as the new label (that is
### what makes an item carry one chain state at a time), and '--dry-run' prints what would be applied
### without writing anything.  Nothing is remembered between calls: what the item carries is read
### fresh, from 'rad issue|patch list', every time.
###
### 'rad_reaction' is the same idea for the reaction markers, and those are a different animal: a
### reaction is *private per identity* and can never be delegated, so there is no trust question here
### -- what the caller compares and swaps is its *own* state, reduced the way the store reduces it:
### the newest op per target and emoji of ours, with 'active' read off that survivor, ordered by the
### op's position in the chain (an add and its undo land in the same second routinely).
###
###   bar rad_reaction [--from <emoji>] [--repo <rid|path>] <to> <target>..
###   bar rad_reaction <from> <to> <target>.. [--repo <rid|path>]
###
### '<to>' is the marker to set: one emoji of the store's allow list, checked before any call, so the
### '❤️' an emoji picker hands out (heart plus a variation selector) is refused here instead of by the
### store.  A target is '<issue-id>' (the issue itself), '<issue-id> <comment-id>',
### '<patch-or-revision-id>' (a patch id is its root revision) or '<patch-or-revision-id>
### <comment-id>'.
###
### '<from>' is what the caller expects to be live there: omitted is no expectation at all, the empty
### string is 'no live marker of mine', anything else must be the state -- and a state which does not
### match fails without writing anything.  A patch level transition takes '<from>' back in the same
### step ('rad patch react --undo'), so the target ends with one live marker; the issue level cannot
### ('rad issue react' has no '--undo' in rad 1.10.3), so there the transition is supersede-only and
### the check is a guard, never a cleanup.  An empty '<to>' takes '<from>' back and sets nothing:
### patch level only, and it needs '--from', because the expectation is what names the marker.
###
### 'rad_topic' opens the topic tree of that work in one action: it mints the name from the
### branch *class* and the description, resolves the clone and the freshest base, keeps '.worktrees/'
### out of the main checkout's status and runs 'git worktree add' -- the sequence an agent otherwise
### reassembles by hand every time it starts a topic:
###
###   bar rad_topic fix "crash on empty config"
###   /home/ct/hermes_src/bar/.worktrees/fix-crash-on-empty-config  fix-crash-on-empty-config  base=rad/main  head=55d5924
###
### The name is '<class>-<description>'.  The class vocabulary is read from the branch matchers of
### the 'git' module ('is_git_*_branch'), so bar's own classes and the gates it hangs off them stay
### the one source of truth, and a topic's name says what kind of change it is from anywhere -- the
### worktree, the branch and the patch head.  A name without a class is refused, never guessed.
### <description> is slugified to '[a-z0-9._-]' and the whole name stays within 40 characters, the
### limit the Hermes worktree machinery sanitises to; a description which cannot fit is refused, never
### silently cut.
###
### The clone is the *main checkout* of the repository '--root' names, or the one the current
### directory is in -- a linked worktree resolves to its clone, so the call works from inside any
### topic.  The base is the clone's *trunk*: on this host the trunk is upstream plus the local work
### which is not published yet, so branching off the remote tip would drop that work.  The 'rad'
### remote (else 'origin') is therefore fetched first -- to *know* the relation -- and a trunk which
### is behind upstream is refused, because updating the trunk is its own step with its own gate and
### never a side effect of opening a topic.  '--base <ref>' overrides the base, '--no-fetch' skips
### the fetch.
###
### Neither of the two rules above writes to the main checkout: the tree is created below
### '<clone>/.worktrees/', kept out of 'git status' by a line in '.git/info/exclude' (never by the
### tracked '.gitignore'), and 'git worktree add' does not touch the trunk's HEAD.
###
### 'rad_import' is the way in, not a question: on the toplevel of a git repository which is not on
### radicle yet it runs 'rad init' (public, signing set up) and pushes every branch to the new
### remote.  Once a 'rad' remote exists it does nothing but say so.
###
### Running 'rad', reading its tables back, the age of a last event and the COB store are shared with
### every other module of bar which talks to radicle and live in Bar.d/radicle_lib, which this module
### requires: 'RAD_BIN' (the command to run instead of 'rad'), the record separators and every 'rad_*'
### function come from there.  The JSON literals of the '--json' forms below come from
### Bar.d/json_lib, the one place where bar writes them.
###
### 'RAD_STATE_MAX' is the default of '--max'.  Reading rad's tables back needs a character aware
### shell; bar exports a UTF-8 'LC_ALL' whenever the system has one.
###
### The rules below are about 'rad', the radicle frontend and the repository it is used in.  Building
### a radicle installation itself is not one of them (radicle is packaged; the tools below
### 'tools/radicle_*' are their own thing).

# helper-module: rad

require radicle_lib json_lib

# helper: rad_import | import a git repository into radicle: 'rad init' and push every branch to the new remote | rad_import | nothing; a notice when the repository is already on radicle
## Imports the repository at the toplevel.  Not a question but the way in: it changes the repository
## (a new remote, the branches pushed) and says what it did.
rule rad_import: is_git_toplevel -

# helper: rad_state | the radicle state of this checkout in one invocation | rad_state [--json] [--max <n>] | one '<section>  <counts>' line and the items per section; '--json' the same as one object
## The radicle state of this checkout: repository, identity, patches, issues and the branches
## which still carry local work.
rule rad_state: -

# helper: rad_patches | the patches filtered by state and author | rad_patches [--json] [--max <n>] [--state <one of open, draft, merged, archived, all>] [--author <one of ours, other, all>] | the patch section of 'rad_state', filtered; '--json' the same as one object
## The patches of this checkout, by state and by author.
rule rad_patches: -

# helper: rad_trust | whether the author of an incoming Radicle issue or patch counts as a trusted source, that is a delegate or an identity with at least <threshold> authored commits | rad_trust --repo <rid-or-path> --author <did-or-nid-or-alias> [--threshold <n>] [--json] | one '<verdict> author=… delegate=… authored=… threshold=… reason=… repo=…' line, <verdict> one of trusted, untrusted, unknown; '--json' the same facts as one object
## The trust verdict of the triage policy; exit 0 trusted, 1 untrusted, 2 could not answer.
rule rad_trust: -

# helper: rad_comment_metadata | the structured metadata the comments of one item carry at their start ('*label:', '*assign:', '*unassign:'), authenticated against trust | rad_comment_metadata <item> --query <label,assign,unassign> [--repo <rid,path>] [--trust <spec>].. [--last] [--json] | one '<query> item=<issue,patch>:<id> author=<alias> did=<did> trust=<delegate,named,assignee,contributor(<n>)> date=<iso> value=<value>' line per trusted entry, oldest first; '--json' the same as one object, plus the item's assignees
## The trusted '*label:'/'*assign:'/'*unassign:' proposals of an item's comments; exit 0 answered,
## 2 usage, 3 no storage for that repository.
##
## Who is trusted is read when the question is asked, from the repository (its delegates), from the
## item (the identities it is assigned to) and from the command line ('--trust'); 'contributor(<n>)'
## asks 'RAD_TRUST_CMD' for the commits that identity authored as itself -- the gate resolves into
## radicle identities, commits of plain git authors are not counted.  Only identities of the radicle
## system carry trust -- an op whose author or assignee is not a DID/NID is dropped, silently, and
## 'RAD_TRUST_CMD' being unset is only an error when a contributor threshold was asked for.  A
## proposal which is not trusted yet can become trusted later (a delegate assigns the item to its
## author), so the answer to the same query is allowed to change between runs.
rule rad_comment_metadata: -

# helper: rad_topic | open the topic tree of an incoming item: mint the '<class>-<description>' name, resolve the clone and the freshest base, keep '.worktrees/' out of 'git status' and create the worktree | rad_topic <class> <description> [--root <clone>] [--base <ref>] [--no-fetch] [--dry-run] [--json] | one '<path>  <branch>  base=<base>  head=<sha>' line; '--json' the same as one object
## Creates '<clone>/.worktrees/<class>-<description>' on the branch of the same name; exit 0 created,
## 1 refused (the tree or the branch exists, the name is too long), 2 could not answer (usage, an
## unknown class, not a git repository, git failed).
rule rad_topic: -

# --- rad_import: getting a repository onto radicle ----------------------------------------------


function rad_import ## import a git repository into radicle
{
    if ! git remote | grep '^rad$'; then
        rad init --name "${PWD##*/}" --description "" --public --setup-signing --no-confirm -v
        git push --all rad
    else
        note "radicle already initialized"
    fi
}


# --- the state of a checkout: rad_state and rad_patches -----------------------------------------

declare -gx RAD_STATE_MAX="${RAD_STATE_MAX:-10}"   ## default of '--max': items printed per section

declare -ga RAD_STATE_PATCHES=()   ## one record per patch, see 'rad_state_collect'
declare -ga RAD_STATE_ISSUES=()    ## one record per issue, see 'rad_state_collect'
declare -ga RAD_STATE_BRANCHES=()  ## one record per local branch, see 'rad_state_collect'
declare -gA RAD_STATE_COB_TIME=()  ## '<type>:<id>' -> '<updated> <created> <kind>'
declare -g RAD_STATE_RID="" RAD_STATE_NAME="" RAD_STATE_PATH=""
declare -g RAD_STATE_ALIAS="" RAD_STATE_DID="" RAD_STATE_KNOWN="no"
declare -g RAD_STATE_BRANCH="" RAD_STATE_COMMIT="" RAD_STATE_CANONICAL=""
declare -g RAD_STATE_CANONICAL_COMMIT="" RAD_STATE_AHEAD="" RAD_STATE_BEHIND="" RAD_STATE_NOTCANONICAL=""
declare -gi RAD_STATE_CUT=0        ## items the last text rendering elided
declare -gi RAD_STATE_NOW=0        ## the timestamp the ages are relative to
## - 0 when this shell counts characters, which reading rad's tables needs
function rad_state_charwise
{
    ## 'rad' pads the cells of its tables by character, so the columns can only be cut at the same
    ## offsets when the shell measures the same way.  That is the case in a UTF-8 locale, which bar
    ## exports whenever the system has one; in a byte locale a cell holding a non ASCII character
    ## shifts everything behind it and 'rad_state' says so instead of reporting it wrongly.
    ## (A double width character -- CJK, emoji -- is one column to a character wise shell and two
    ## to rad: such a title would shift the columns of its row too.)
    local probe="…"
    [[ "${#probe}" == 1 ]]
}

## <table> - the ids of the rows of a 'rad' table, one per line
function rad_state_ids
{
    ## A state filter is answered with a table of the same shape and only the ids are taken from
    ## it.  An empty result is 'Nothing to show.' or nothing at all -- that is no id, not an
    ## error; box drawing characters without a header to cut by are an error (return 1).
    local table="$1"
    local ids
    [[ "$table" == *│* ]] || return 0
    ids="$(rad_table "$table" ID)" || return 1
    [[ -n "$ids" ]] || return 0
    printf '%s\n' "$ids"
}

## <newline separated ids> <id> - 0 when <id> is one of them
function rad_state_has_id
{
    local ids="${1//$'\n'/ }"
    [[ " $ids " == *" $2 "* ]]
}

## <ids> <description> <table> - as 'rad_state_ids', but a table it cannot cut is a diagnostic
function rad_state_ids_checked
{
    local description="$1" table="$2"
    local ids=""
    ids="$(rad_state_ids "$table")" || {
        error "cannot read what '$RAD_BIN $description' printed: a table without the expected columns"
        return 1
    }
    printf '%s' "$ids"
}

## <description> <table> <column>.. - as 'rad_table', but a table it cannot cut is a diagnostic
function rad_state_table_checked
{
    local description="$1" table="$2"
    shift 2
    local rows=""
    ## 'Nothing to show.' and nothing at all are no rows, not an error; box drawing characters
    ## without a header to cut by are.
    [[ "$table" == *│* ]] || return 0
    rows="$(rad_table "$table" "$@")" || {
        error "cannot read what '$RAD_BIN $description' printed: a table without the expected columns"
        return 1
    }
    printf '%s' "$rows"
}

## <cell> - an author cell of a table as one name ('ct_vibes  (you)' -> 'ct_vibes')
function rad_state_author
{
    local cell="${1//$'\t'/ }"
    while [[ "$cell" == *"  "* ]]; do
        cell="${cell//  / }"
    done
    printf '%s' "${cell% (you)}"
}

## - collect the whole state into the 'RAD_STATE_*' variables
function rad_state_collect
{
    ##   patch  id state own author head title updated created kind
    ##   issue  id state own author labels assignees title updated created kind
    ##   branch name commit ahead patch
    ##
    ## Returns 3 when there is no repository to report on (a radicle repository is a git
    ## repository with a radicle identity), 1 when rad failed or its output was not understood.
    local toplevel="" rid="" all="" ours="" draft="" merged="" archived="" closed="" solved=""
    local myself="" inspected="" ref=""
    local id="" title="" author="" head="" state="" own="" labels="" assignees=""
    local draftids="" mergedids="" archivedids="" closedids="" solvedids="" rows=""
    local -a heads=() headmap=()
    local type="" name="" branch="" commit="" ahead="" patch="" full="" entry="" object=""
    local updated="" kind=""

    RAD_STATE_PATCHES=() RAD_STATE_ISSUES=() RAD_STATE_BRANCHES=() RAD_STATE_COB_TIME=()
    RAD_STATE_CUT=0

    if ! toplevel="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)"; then
        error "not a git repository: 'bar rad_state' reports on a radicle checkout"
        return 3
    fi
    RAD_STATE_NAME="${toplevel##*/}"
    RAD_STATE_PATH="${toplevel/#"$HOME"/\~}"

    inspected="$(cd "$toplevel" && rad_run inspect --rid)" || return $?
    rid="${inspected##*$'\n'}"
    RAD_STATE_RID="$rid"

    RAD_STATE_ALIAS="" RAD_STATE_DID="" RAD_STATE_KNOWN="no"
    myself="$(LC_ALL=C "$RAD_BIN" self 2>/dev/null)" || myself=""
    if [[ -n "$myself" ]]; then
        RAD_STATE_ALIAS="$(printf '%s\n' "$myself" | LC_ALL=C sed -n 's/^Alias[[:space:]]\{1,\}//p' | head -1)"
        RAD_STATE_DID="$(printf '%s\n' "$myself" | LC_ALL=C sed -n 's/^DID[[:space:]]\{1,\}//p' | head -1)"
        [[ -n "$RAD_STATE_DID" ]] && RAD_STATE_KNOWN="yes"
    else
        warn "'$RAD_BIN self' failed, the identity rad uses here stays unknown"
    fi

    RAD_STATE_BRANCH="$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null)" || RAD_STATE_BRANCH="HEAD"
    RAD_STATE_COMMIT="$(git rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null)" || RAD_STATE_COMMIT=""
    RAD_STATE_CANONICAL="" RAD_STATE_CANONICAL_COMMIT=""
    RAD_STATE_AHEAD="" RAD_STATE_BEHIND="" RAD_STATE_NOTCANONICAL=""
    for ref in refs/remotes/rad/main refs/remotes/rad/master; do
        if git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$ref" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            RAD_STATE_CANONICAL="${ref#refs/remotes/}"
            RAD_STATE_CANONICAL_COMMIT="$(git rev-parse --short "$ref")"
            read -r RAD_STATE_BEHIND RAD_STATE_AHEAD <<<"$(git rev-list --left-right --count "$ref...HEAD" 2>/dev/null)" ||
                RAD_STATE_BEHIND=""
            RAD_STATE_NOTCANONICAL="$(git rev-list --count --branches --not "$ref" 2>/dev/null)" ||
                RAD_STATE_NOTCANONICAL=""
            break
        fi
    done

    for type in patch issue; do
        rad_cobs_time "$type" "$rid" RAD_STATE_COB_TIME
    done

    rad_state_charwise ||
        warn "the shell does not count characters ('LC_ALL=${LC_ALL:-unset}'): cells with non ASCII characters may be cut wrongly"

    # the patches: the states and our own ones as id sets, the rest from the table of '--all'
    all="$(rad_run patch list --repo "$rid" --all)" || return $?
    ours="$(rad_run patch list --repo "$rid" --all --authored)" || return $?
    draft="$(rad_run patch list --repo "$rid" --draft)" || return $?
    merged="$(rad_run patch list --repo "$rid" --merged)" || return $?
    archived="$(rad_run patch list --repo "$rid" --archived)" || return $?
    draftids="$(rad_state_ids_checked "patch list --draft" "$draft")" || return 1
    mergedids="$(rad_state_ids_checked "patch list --merged" "$merged")" || return 1
    archivedids="$(rad_state_ids_checked "patch list --archived" "$archived")" || return 1
    ours="$(rad_state_ids_checked "patch list --all --authored" "$ours")" || return 1
    rows="$(rad_state_table_checked "patch list --all" "$all" ID Title Author Head)" || return 1
    while IFS="$RAD_US" read -r id title author head; do
        [[ -n "$id" ]] || continue
        own="other"
        rad_state_has_id "$ours" "$id" && own="ours"
        if rad_state_has_id "$draftids" "$id"; then
            state="draft"
        elif rad_state_has_id "$mergedids" "$id"; then
            state="merged"
        elif rad_state_has_id "$archivedids" "$id"; then
            state="archived"
        else
            state="open"
        fi
        updated="" kind=""
        read -r updated kind <<<"${RAD_STATE_COB_TIME["patch:$id"]:-}"
        RAD_STATE_PATCHES+=( "$id${RAD_US}$state${RAD_US}$own${RAD_US}$(rad_state_author "$author")${RAD_US}$head${RAD_US}$title${RAD_US}$updated${RAD_US}$kind" )
        heads+=( "$head${RAD_US}$id" )
    done <<<"$rows"

    # the heads resolved once, so that every local branch is compared without another git call
    for entry in ${heads[@]+"${heads[@]}"}; do
        full="$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet "${entry%%"$RAD_US"*}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null)" || continue
        [[ -n "$full" ]] && headmap+=( "$full${RAD_US}${entry##*"$RAD_US"}" )
    done

    # the issues
    all="$(rad_run issue list --repo "$rid" --all)" || return $?
    closed="$(rad_run issue list --repo "$rid" --closed)" || return $?
    solved="$(rad_run issue list --repo "$rid" --solved)" || return $?
    closedids="$(rad_state_ids_checked "issue list --closed" "$closed")" || return 1
    solvedids="$(rad_state_ids_checked "issue list --solved" "$solved")" || return 1
    rows="$(rad_state_table_checked "issue list --all" "$all" ID Title Author Labels Assignees)" ||
        return 1
    while IFS="$RAD_US" read -r id title author labels assignees; do
        [[ -n "$id" ]] || continue
        own="other"
        [[ "$author" == *"(you)"* ]] && own="ours"
        if rad_state_has_id "$closedids" "$id"; then
            state="closed"
        elif rad_state_has_id "$solvedids" "$id"; then
            state="solved"
        else
            state="open"
        fi
        updated="" kind=""
        read -r updated kind <<<"${RAD_STATE_COB_TIME["issue:$id"]:-}"
        RAD_STATE_ISSUES+=( "$id${RAD_US}$state${RAD_US}$own${RAD_US}$(rad_state_author "$author")${RAD_US}$labels${RAD_US}$assignees${RAD_US}$title${RAD_US}$updated${RAD_US}$kind" )
    done <<<"$rows"

    # the branches: one carries a patch when its tip is the head of one
    while IFS="$RAD_RS" read -r branch commit; do
        [[ -n "$branch" ]] || continue
        ahead=""
        if [[ -n "$RAD_STATE_CANONICAL" ]]; then
            ahead="$(git rev-list --count "$RAD_STATE_CANONICAL..$branch" 2>/dev/null)" || ahead=""
        fi
        patch="-"
        full="$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$commit^{commit}" 2>/dev/null)" || full=""
        if [[ -n "$full" ]]; then
            for entry in ${headmap[@]+"${headmap[@]}"}; do
                [[ "${entry%%"$RAD_US"*}" == "$full" ]] || continue
                patch="${entry##*"$RAD_US"}"
                break
            done
        fi
        RAD_STATE_BRANCHES+=( "$branch${RAD_US}$commit${RAD_US}$ahead${RAD_US}$patch" )
    done < <(git for-each-ref --format="%(refname:short)${RAD_RS}%(objectname:short)" refs/heads)
}

## <shown> <total> - the elision of a cut section, nothing when every item is shown
function rad_state_cut
{
    local shown="$1" total="$2"
    (( shown < total )) || return 0
    RAD_STATE_CUT=$(( RAD_STATE_CUT + total - shown ))
    printf '  ... %d of %d elided (%s %s --max 0)\n' "$(( total - shown ))" "$total" \
        "${BAR_CALLED_AS:-bar}" "${RULE_NAME:-rad_state}"
}

## - where the complete text of this run is written, see 'rad_state_complete_note'
function rad_state_complete_file
{
    printf '%s' "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/bar-rad_state.${RAD_STATE_RID//[!A-Za-z0-9]/_}.${RULE_NAME:-rad_state}.txt"
}

## <file> - say where the complete text went, after a section was cut
function rad_state_complete_note
{
    printf 'complete  %s  (the same text, nothing elided)\n' "$1"
}

## <max> [<state> [<author>]] - the patch section as text
function rad_state_text_patches
{
    local max="$1" select_state="${2:-all}" select_author="${3:-all}"
    local total=${#RAD_STATE_PATCHES[@]} shown=0 ours=0 others=0
    local open=0 draft=0 merged=0 archived=0
    local entry="" id="" state="" own="" author="" head="" title="" updated="" kind="" suffix=""
    local -a selected=()

    for entry in ${RAD_STATE_PATCHES[@]+"${RAD_STATE_PATCHES[@]}"}; do
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r id state own author head title updated kind <<<"$entry"
        if [[ "$own" == ours ]]; then
            ours=$(( ours + 1 ))
        else
            others=$(( others + 1 ))
        fi
        case "$state" in
        open) open=$(( open + 1 )) ;;
        draft) draft=$(( draft + 1 )) ;;
        merged) merged=$(( merged + 1 )) ;;
        archived) archived=$(( archived + 1 )) ;;
        esac
        [[ "$select_state" == all || "$select_state" == "$state" ]] || continue
        [[ "$select_author" == all || "$select_author" == "$own" ]] || continue
        selected+=( "$entry" )
    done

    if [[ "$select_state" == all && "$select_author" == all ]]; then
        printf 'patches   %d   ours %d  others %d   open %d  draft %d  merged %d  archived %d\n' \
            "$total" "$ours" "$others" "$open" "$draft" "$merged" "$archived"
    else
        printf 'patches   %d of %d   ours %d  others %d   open %d  draft %d  merged %d  archived %d   (state %s, author %s)\n' \
            "${#selected[@]}" "$total" "$ours" "$others" "$open" "$draft" "$merged" "$archived" \
            "$select_state" "$select_author"
    fi
    for entry in ${selected[@]+"${selected[@]}"}; do
        (( max == 0 || shown < max )) || break
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r id state own author head title updated kind <<<"$entry"
        ## the author of a patch of ours is us, naming it again would only repeat the identity
        ## line; for the others it says who to talk to
        suffix=""
        [[ "$own" == other && -n "$author" ]] && suffix="  [${author}]"
        printf '  %s  %-8s %-5s  %-7s %-4s %s%s\n' "$id" "$state" "$own" "$head" \
            "$(rad_age "$RAD_STATE_NOW" "$updated")" "$title" "$suffix"
        shown=$(( shown + 1 ))
    done
    rad_state_cut "$shown" "${#selected[@]}"
}

## <max> - the issue section as text
function rad_state_text_issues
{
    local max="$1"
    local total=${#RAD_STATE_ISSUES[@]} shown=0 ours=0 others=0 open=0 closed=0 solved=0
    local entry="" id="" state="" own="" author="" labels="" assignees="" title="" updated="" kind="" suffix=""

    for entry in ${RAD_STATE_ISSUES[@]+"${RAD_STATE_ISSUES[@]}"}; do
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r id state own author labels assignees title updated kind <<<"$entry"
        if [[ "$own" == ours ]]; then
            ours=$(( ours + 1 ))
        else
            others=$(( others + 1 ))
        fi
        case "$state" in
        open) open=$(( open + 1 )) ;;
        closed) closed=$(( closed + 1 )) ;;
        solved) solved=$(( solved + 1 )) ;;
        esac
    done
    printf 'issues    %d   ours %d  others %d   open %d  closed %d  solved %d\n' \
        "$total" "$ours" "$others" "$open" "$closed" "$solved"
    for entry in ${RAD_STATE_ISSUES[@]+"${RAD_STATE_ISSUES[@]}"}; do
        (( max == 0 || shown < max )) || break
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r id state own author labels assignees title updated kind <<<"$entry"
        suffix=""
        [[ -n "$labels" ]] && suffix+="  [${labels}]"
        [[ -n "$assignees" ]] && suffix+="  [@${assignees}]"
        printf '  %s  %-6s %-5s  %-4s %s%s\n' "$id" "$state" "$own" \
            "$(rad_age "$RAD_STATE_NOW" "$updated")" "$title" "$suffix"
        shown=$(( shown + 1 ))
    done
    rad_state_cut "$shown" "$total"
}

## <max> - the branch section as text
function rad_state_text_branches
{
    local max="$1"
    local total=${#RAD_STATE_BRANCHES[@]} shown=0 without=0
    local entry="" name="" commit="" ahead="" patch=""

    for entry in ${RAD_STATE_BRANCHES[@]+"${RAD_STATE_BRANCHES[@]}"}; do
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r name commit ahead patch <<<"$entry"
        [[ "$patch" == "-" ]] && without=$(( without + 1 ))
    done
    printf 'branches  %d   no patch %d   %s commits not in %s\n' "$total" "$without" \
        "${RAD_STATE_NOTCANONICAL:-?}" "${RAD_STATE_CANONICAL:-rad/main}"
    for entry in ${RAD_STATE_BRANCHES[@]+"${RAD_STATE_BRANCHES[@]}"}; do
        (( max == 0 || shown < max )) || break
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r name commit ahead patch <<<"$entry"
        if [[ "$patch" == "-" ]]; then
            patch="no patch"
        else
            patch="patch $patch"
        fi
        printf '  %-28s %-7s %-8s %s\n' "$name" "$commit" "${ahead:+ahead $ahead}" "$patch"
        shown=$(( shown + 1 ))
    done
    rad_state_cut "$shown" "$total"
}

## - the repository, identity and head lines of the text form
function rad_state_text_head
{
    printf 'repo      %s  %s  %s\n' "$RAD_STATE_NAME" "$RAD_STATE_RID" "$RAD_STATE_PATH"
    if [[ "$RAD_STATE_KNOWN" == yes ]]; then
        printf 'ident     %s  %s\n' "${RAD_STATE_ALIAS:-?}" "$RAD_STATE_DID"
    else
        printf 'ident     unknown  (rad self did not answer)\n'
    fi
    if [[ -n "$RAD_STATE_CANONICAL" ]]; then
        printf 'head      %s %s   %s %s   ahead %s  behind %s  (as of the last fetch)\n' \
            "$RAD_STATE_BRANCH" "$RAD_STATE_COMMIT" "$RAD_STATE_CANONICAL" \
            "$RAD_STATE_CANONICAL_COMMIT" "${RAD_STATE_AHEAD:-?}" "${RAD_STATE_BEHIND:-?}"
    else
        printf 'head      %s %s   no rad/main as a base (run '\''git fetch rad'\'')\n' \
            "$RAD_STATE_BRANCH" "$RAD_STATE_COMMIT"
    fi
}

## <max> - the whole state as text, at most <max> items per section
function rad_state_text
{
    local max="$1"
    local file=""

    RAD_STATE_CUT=0
    rad_state_text_head
    rad_state_text_patches "$max" all all
    rad_state_text_issues "$max"
    rad_state_text_branches "$max"
    if (( max != 0 && RAD_STATE_CUT != 0 )); then
        file="$(rad_state_complete_file)"
        if rad_state_text 0 >"$file" 2>/dev/null; then
            rad_state_complete_note "$file"
        else
            warn "could not write the complete text to $file"
        fi
    fi
}

## <state> <author> - the selected patches as one JSON object
function rad_state_json_patches
{
    local select_state="$1" select_author="$2"
    local entry="" id="" state="" own="" author="" head="" title=""
    local updated="" kind="" joined="" object=""
    local total=0 ours=0 others=0 open=0 draft=0 merged=0 archived=0

    for entry in ${RAD_STATE_PATCHES[@]+"${RAD_STATE_PATCHES[@]}"}; do
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r id state own author head title updated kind <<<"$entry"
        total=$(( total + 1 ))
        if [[ "$own" == ours ]]; then
            ours=$(( ours + 1 ))
        else
            others=$(( others + 1 ))
        fi
        case "$state" in
        open) open=$(( open + 1 )) ;;
        draft) draft=$(( draft + 1 )) ;;
        merged) merged=$(( merged + 1 )) ;;
        archived) archived=$(( archived + 1 )) ;;
        esac
        [[ "$select_state" == all || "$select_state" == "$state" ]] || continue
        [[ "$select_author" == all || "$select_author" == "$own" ]] || continue
        object="$(printf '{"id":%s,"state":%s,"author":%s,"ours":%s,"title":%s,"head":%s,"updated":%s,"age":%s,"last":%s}' \
            "$(json_string "$id")" "$(json_string "$state")" \
            "$(json_string "$author")" "$(json_bool "$([[ "$own" == ours ]] && printf yes || printf no)")" \
            "$(json_string "$title")" "$(json_string "$head")" \
            "$(json_number "$updated")" \
            "$(json_string "$(rad_age "$RAD_STATE_NOW" "$updated")")" \
            "$(json_string "$kind")")"
        joined+="${joined:+,}${object}"
    done
    printf '{"count":%d,"ours":%d,"others":%d,"open":%d,"draft":%d,"merged":%d,"archived":%d,"items":[%s]}' \
        "$total" "$ours" "$others" "$open" "$draft" "$merged" "$archived" "$joined"
}

## - the issues as one JSON object
function rad_state_json_issues
{
    local entry="" id="" state="" own="" author="" labels="" assignees="" title=""
    local updated="" kind="" joined="" object=""
    local total=0 ours=0 others=0 open=0 closed=0 solved=0

    for entry in ${RAD_STATE_ISSUES[@]+"${RAD_STATE_ISSUES[@]}"}; do
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r id state own author labels assignees title updated kind <<<"$entry"
        total=$(( total + 1 ))
        if [[ "$own" == ours ]]; then
            ours=$(( ours + 1 ))
        else
            others=$(( others + 1 ))
        fi
        case "$state" in
        open) open=$(( open + 1 )) ;;
        closed) closed=$(( closed + 1 )) ;;
        solved) solved=$(( solved + 1 )) ;;
        esac
        object="$(printf '{"id":%s,"state":%s,"author":%s,"ours":%s,"labels":%s,"assignees":%s,"title":%s,"updated":%s,"age":%s,"last":%s}' \
            "$(json_string "$id")" "$(json_string "$state")" \
            "$(json_string "$author")" "$(json_bool "$([[ "$own" == ours ]] && printf yes || printf no)")" \
            "$(json_list "$labels")" "$(json_list "$assignees")" \
            "$(json_string "$title")" "$(json_number "$updated")" \
            "$(json_string "$(rad_age "$RAD_STATE_NOW" "$updated")")" \
            "$(json_string "$kind")")"
        joined+="${joined:+,}${object}"
    done
    printf '{"count":%d,"ours":%d,"others":%d,"open":%d,"closed":%d,"solved":%d,"items":[%s]}' \
        "$total" "$ours" "$others" "$open" "$closed" "$solved" "$joined"
}

## - the local branches as one JSON object
function rad_state_json_branches
{
    local entry="" name="" commit="" ahead="" patch=""
    local joined="" object=""
    local total=0 without=0

    for entry in ${RAD_STATE_BRANCHES[@]+"${RAD_STATE_BRANCHES[@]}"}; do
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r name commit ahead patch <<<"$entry"
        total=$(( total + 1 ))
        [[ "$patch" == "-" ]] && without=$(( without + 1 ))
        if [[ "$patch" == "-" ]]; then
            patch="null"
        else
            patch="$(json_string "$patch")"
        fi
        object="$(printf '{"name":%s,"commit":%s,"ahead":%s,"patch":%s}' \
            "$(json_string "$name")" "$(json_string "$commit")" \
            "$(json_number "$ahead")" "$patch")"
        joined+="${joined:+,}${object}"
    done
    printf '{"count":%d,"without_patch":%d,"not_in_canonical":%s,"items":[%s]}' \
        "$total" "$without" "$(json_number "$RAD_STATE_NOTCANONICAL")" "$joined"
}

## <state> <author> - the complete state as one JSON object
function rad_state_json
{
    printf '{"bar_rad":1,"repo":{"rid":%s,"name":%s,"path":%s},"identity":{"alias":%s,"did":%s,"known":%s},"head":{"branch":%s,"commit":%s,"canonical":%s,"canonical_commit":%s,"ahead":%s,"behind":%s},"select":{"state":%s,"author":%s},"patches":' \
        "$(json_string "$RAD_STATE_RID")" "$(json_string "$RAD_STATE_NAME")" \
        "$(json_string "$RAD_STATE_PATH")" "$(json_string "$RAD_STATE_ALIAS")" \
        "$(json_string "$RAD_STATE_DID")" "$(json_bool "$RAD_STATE_KNOWN")" \
        "$(json_string "$RAD_STATE_BRANCH")" "$(json_string "$RAD_STATE_COMMIT")" \
        "$(json_string "$RAD_STATE_CANONICAL")" "$(json_string "$RAD_STATE_CANONICAL_COMMIT")" \
        "$(json_number "$RAD_STATE_AHEAD")" "$(json_number "$RAD_STATE_BEHIND")" \
        "$(json_string "$1")" "$(json_string "$2")"
    rad_state_json_patches "$1" "$2"
    printf ',"issues":'
    rad_state_json_issues
    printf ',"branches":'
    rad_state_json_branches
    printf '}\n'
}

## - the usage of both rules, on stderr
function rad_state_usage
{
    printf 'usage: %s rad_state [--json] [--max <n>]\n' "${BAR_CALLED_AS:-bar}" >&2
    printf '       %s rad_patches [--json] [--max <n>] [--state <open|draft|merged|archived|all>] [--author <ours|other|all>]\n' \
        "${BAR_CALLED_AS:-bar}" >&2
}

## [--json] [--max <n>] - the whole state, see the module header
function rad_state
{
    local json="" max="$RAD_STATE_MAX" arg="" i=0
    local -a args=( ${RULE_ARGS[@]+"${RULE_ARGS[@]}"} )

    while (( i < ${#args[@]} )); do
        arg="${args[i]}"
        case "$arg" in
        -h | --help)
            ## 'rad_state_usage' reports to stderr for a usage error; as the answer to '--help' the
            ## same text is the output, so its stream is folded over.
            rad_state_usage 2>&1
            return 0
            ;;
        --json)
            json=1
            ;;
        --max)
            i=$(( i + 1 ))
            max="${args[i]:-}"
            ;;
        *)
            rad_state_usage
            return 2
            ;;
        esac
        i=$(( i + 1 ))
    done
    [[ "$max" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || {
        rad_state_usage
        return 2
    }
    [[ -n "${BAR_AGENT_JSON:-}" ]] && json=1

    is_command_installed "$RAD_BIN" || {
        error "'$RAD_BIN' is not installed"
        return 1
    }
    rad_state_collect || return $?
    RAD_STATE_NOW="$(date +%s)"

    if [[ -n "$json" ]]; then
        rad_state_json all all
    else
        rad_state_text "$max"
    fi
}

## [--json] [--max <n>] [--state <s>] [--author <a>] - the patches, see the module header
function rad_patches
{
    local json="" max="$RAD_STATE_MAX" select_state="all" select_author="all" arg="" i=0
    local -a args=( ${RULE_ARGS[@]+"${RULE_ARGS[@]}"} )

    while (( i < ${#args[@]} )); do
        arg="${args[i]}"
        case "$arg" in
        -h | --help)
            rad_state_usage 2>&1
            return 0
            ;;
        --json)
            json=1
            ;;
        --max | --state | --author)
            i=$(( i + 1 ))
            case "$arg" in
            --max) max="${args[i]:-}" ;;
            --state) select_state="${args[i]:-}" ;;
            --author) select_author="${args[i]:-}" ;;
            esac
            ;;
        *)
            rad_state_usage
            return 2
            ;;
        esac
        i=$(( i + 1 ))
    done
    case "$select_state" in
    all | open | draft | merged | archived) ;;
    *)
        rad_state_usage
        return 2
        ;;
    esac
    case "$select_author" in
    all | ours | other) ;;
    *)
        rad_state_usage
        return 2
        ;;
    esac
    [[ "$max" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || {
        rad_state_usage
        return 2
    }
    [[ -n "${BAR_AGENT_JSON:-}" ]] && json=1

    is_command_installed "$RAD_BIN" || {
        error "'$RAD_BIN' is not installed"
        return 1
    }
    rad_state_collect || return $?
    RAD_STATE_NOW="$(date +%s)"

    if [[ -n "$json" ]]; then
        rad_state_json "$select_state" "$select_author"
    else
        local file=""
        rad_state_text_head
        rad_state_text_patches "$max" "$select_state" "$select_author"
        if (( max != 0 && RAD_STATE_CUT != 0 )); then
            file="$(rad_state_complete_file)"
            if { rad_state_text_head; rad_state_text_patches 0 "$select_state" "$select_author"; } >"$file" 2>/dev/null; then
                rad_state_complete_note "$file"
            else
                warn "could not write the complete text to $file"
            fi
        fi
    fi
}

# --- minting the name: the classes bar defines, and the slug of a description --------------------

function rad_classes ## - the branch classes bar defines, one '<class>-' prefix per line
{
    ## Read from the 'is_git_*_branch' rules of the git module, so the vocabulary has one source of
    ## truth: the matcher 'fix-* fix/*' contributes the class 'fix-'.  Bar's matches are regexes, so
    ## 'fix-*' is the prefix 'fix-' followed by anything; the slash forms ('fix/', 'release/') are
    ## not classes of a *topic* here -- this host's layout puts a task id behind the slash
    ## ('<slug>/<task-id>') -- and the bare trunks ('main', 'master', 'devel') have no prefix at all.
    local module="${BAR_DIR:-}/git"
    [[ -f "$module" ]] || return 1
    LC_ALL=C sed -n "s/^rule is_git_[a-z]*_branch: 'git_branch_matches \(.*\)'\$/\1/p" "$module" |
        LC_ALL=C tr ' ' '\n' |
        LC_ALL=C sed -n 's/^\([a-z][a-z]*-\)\*$/\1/p' |
        LC_ALL=C sort -u
}

function rad_slug ## <text> - the description as a name slug, empty when nothing is left
{
    printf '%s' "$1" |
        LC_ALL=C tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' |
        LC_ALL=C sed -e 's/[^a-z0-9._-]\+/-/g' -e 's/--\+/-/g' -e 's/^[-._]\+//' -e 's/[-._]\+$//'
}

# --- rad_trust ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

function rad_trust_usage ## [reason] - usage on stderr
{
    [[ -n "${1:-}" ]] && printf 'rad_trust: %s\n' "$1" >&2
    printf 'usage: rad_trust --repo <rid|path> --author <did|nid|alias> [--threshold <n>] [--json]\n' >&2
}

function rad_trust ## see the module header
{
    ## Body of the 'rad_trust' rule: one verdict line and the exit code which carries it.  The
    ## facts come from the backing tool, this function adds the policy (which is what the triage
    ## skill would otherwise spell out per item).
    local -a args=()
    local raw arg
    local json="" repo="" author="" threshold=""
    local value

    for raw in "$@"; do
        if [[ "$raw" == --*=* ]]; then
            args+=("${raw%%=*}" "${raw#*=}")
        else
            args+=("$raw")
        fi
    done
    local -i i=0 count=${#args[@]}
    while (( i < count )); do
        arg="${args[i]}"
        i=$(( i + 1 ))
        case "$arg" in
        -h | --help)
            rad_trust_usage 2>&1
            return 0
            ;;
        --json) json=1 ;;
        --repo | --author | --threshold)
            if (( i >= count )) || [[ -z "${args[i]}" ]]; then
                rad_trust_usage "'$arg' needs a non-empty value"
                return 2
            fi
            value="${args[i]}"
            i=$(( i + 1 ))
            case "$arg" in
            --repo) repo="$value" ;;
            --author) author="$value" ;;
            --threshold) threshold="$value" ;;
            esac
            ;;
        *)
            rad_trust_usage "unknown argument '$arg'"
            return 2
            ;;
        esac
    done

    [[ -n "${BAR_AGENT_JSON:-}" ]] && json=1
    if [[ -z "$repo" || -z "$author" ]]; then
        rad_trust_usage "both --repo and --author are required"
        return 2
    fi
    if [[ -n "$threshold" && ! "$threshold" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
        rad_trust_usage "invalid --threshold '$threshold', want a non-negative number"
        return 2
    fi

    local tool="${RAD_TRIAGE_FACTS:-$HOME/.hermes/docs/hermes_src/tools/radicle_triage/triage-facts}"
    if [[ ! -x "$tool" ]]; then
        printf 'rad_trust: the fact tool is missing or not executable: %s\n' "$tool" >&2
        return 2
    fi
    if ! command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        printf 'rad_trust: jq is required to read the facts\n' >&2
        return 2
    fi

    local -a cmd=("$tool" --repo "$repo" --author "$author")
    [[ -n "$threshold" ]] && cmd+=(--threshold "$threshold")
    ## The tool exits non-zero when it cannot answer (an unresolvable author is one of those), and
    ## still prints the JSON which says why -- so the exit code is reported, never interpreted: an
    ## empty answer is 'could not answer', anything else is read and the reason travels on.
    local facts tool_rc=0
    facts="$("${cmd[@]}" 2>/dev/null)" || tool_rc=$?
    if [[ -z "$facts" ]]; then
        printf 'rad_trust: %s gave no answer for %s (exit %s)\n' "${tool##*/}" "$repo" "$tool_rc" >&2
        return 2
    fi

    ## One jq pass, so the text form and the machine form carry the same numbers.  An author the
    ## repository cannot resolve is *not* an error of the tool: it has no data, and that is the
    ## 'unknown' verdict (exit 2), never 'untrusted'.  The fields are joined with US (0x1f) and not
    ## with a tab: an empty field has to survive the read, and IFS whitespace would collapse it.
    local fields verdict author_name delegate authored thr reason rid
    if ! fields="$(printf '%s' "$facts" | jq -r '[
            (if .contributor == true then "trusted" elif .contributor == false then "untrusted" else "unknown" end),
            (if .author == null then "-" else (.author.alias // .author.did // "-") end),
            (if .author != null and .author.is_delegate == true then "yes" else "no" end),
            ((.author.total_authored // "-") | tostring),
            ((.threshold // "-") | tostring),
            (([.reason, .error] | map(select(. != null and . != "")) | first // "-") | tostring | gsub("[[:cntrl:]]"; " ")),
            ((.rid // "-") | tostring)
        ] | join("\u001f")' 2>/dev/null)"; then
        printf 'rad_trust: cannot read the facts of %s\n' "${tool##*/}" >&2
        return 2
    fi
    IFS=$'\037' read -r verdict author_name delegate authored thr reason rid <<<"$fields"

    if [[ -n "$json" ]]; then
        printf '%s' "$facts" | jq -c --arg verdict "$verdict" \
            '. + {bar_rad: 1, verdict: $verdict}' 2>/dev/null ||
            printf '{"bar_rad":1,"verdict":%s}\n' "$(json_string "$verdict")"
    else
        printf '%s author=%s delegate=%s authored=%s threshold=%s reason=%s repo=%s\n' \
            "$verdict" "$author_name" "$delegate" "$authored" "$thr" "$reason" "$rid"
    fi

    case "$verdict" in
    trusted) return 0 ;;
    untrusted) return 1 ;;
    *) return 2 ;;
    esac
}

# --- rad_comment_metadata: the metadata a comment carries --------------------------------------

## One record per marker line, oldest first:
##   '<epoch>US<iso>US<type>US<id>US<op>US<alias>US<did>US<mark>US<value>'
## and the same for the entries which survived the trust filter.  For the '--json' form they are
## unpacked again, which is why the shape is fixed here and used nowhere else.
declare -ga RAD_COMMENT_METADATA=()
declare -ga RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_TRUSTED=()
declare -gA RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_AUTHORED=()  ## '<rid>|<nid>' -> the commits that identity authored
declare -g RAD_METADATA_AUTHORED_VALUE=""      ## what 'rad_metadata_authored' hands back
declare -ga RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNEES=()   ## the current assignees of the item asked about
declare -g RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_QUERY=""
declare -g RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_RID=""

function rad_metadata_nid ## <did-or-nid> - the bare NID of an identity
{
    ## 'did:key:z6Mk…', 'did:z6Mk…' and a bare NID all mean the same identity; 'rad' prints the
    ## first form and a human types any of them.
    local value="$1"
    value="${value#did:key:}"
    value="${value#did:}"
    printf '%s' "$value"
}

function rad_metadata_mail_nid ## <mail> - the NID 'rad' writes into a COB op's author mail
{
    ## A COB op is authored as '<alias>@<nid>' (measured: 'cehteh@z6MksaJk…'), so the *actor* of an
    ## op travels in that mail.  The namespace the op was read from does not identify it: one COB's
    ## chain is replicated into every namespace which holds it, byte for byte the same commits.
    local mail="${1##*@}"
    if [[ "$mail" == z6M* ]]; then
        printf '%s' "$mail"
    fi
    return 0
}

function rad_metadata_marks ## <body> - the marker lines at the very start of <body>
{
    ## One '<key>US<value>' record per line of the block.  A marker is '*<key>:<value>' with the key
    ## a word of letters, digits, '_' and '-', starting with a letter -- the '<key>:<value>' header
    ## form and no fixed vocabulary: what a key *means* is the caller's business (this reads any key
    ## back, and only the keys with a registered effect are ever applied).  The block starts at the
    ## comment and ends at the first line which is not a marker: a marker quoted inside a reply is
    ## prose, not a proposal.  A value is a single word -- a label name, a DID, an alias -- and a
    ## line which carries more is dropped rather than handed back half cut.
    local body="$1" line rest key value
    while IFS= read -r line; do
        [[ "$line" == '*'* ]] || break
        rest="${line#\*}"
        [[ "$rest" == *:* ]] || break
        key="${rest%%:*}"
        [[ "$key" =~ ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$ ]] || break
        value="${rest#*:}"
        value="${value#"${value%%[![:space:]]*}"}"
        value="${value%"${value##*[![:space:]]}"}"
        [[ -n "$value" ]] || continue
        [[ "$value" == *[[:space:]]* ]] && continue
        printf '%s%s%s\n' "$key" "$RAD_US" "$value"
    done <<<"$body"
}

function rad_metadata_authored ## <rid> <nid> - the commits that identity authored, '-' when unknown
{
    ## Asked once per identity and remembered, so several thresholds cost one lookup.
    ##
    ## Counted is only what that identity authored *as itself* ('<alias>@<nid>'): the gate has to
    ## resolve into a radicle identity, and commits of plain git authors carry no radicle identity
    ## and are therefore dropped.  That is why 'commits_as_identity' is read and not the wider
    ## 'total_authored' -- an identity the command cannot resolve stays '-' and is not trusted.
    local rid="$1" nid="$2"
    local key="$rid|$nid"
    if [[ -n "${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_AUTHORED[$key]+set}" ]]; then
        RAD_METADATA_AUTHORED_VALUE="${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_AUTHORED[$key]}"
        return 0
    fi
    ## 'RAD_TRUST_CMD' is provided by the deployment (a command and its arguments, e.g.
    ## 'please rad_trust'): where the facts about an identity come from is not this module's
    ## business, and a home directory path does not belong into a vendored source file.
    local -a cmd=()
    local facts="" authored="-"
    read -r -a cmd <<<"${RAD_TRUST_CMD:-}"
    if (( ${#cmd[@]} )) && facts="$("${cmd[@]}" --repo "$rid" --author "$nid" 2>/dev/null)"; then
        authored="$(printf '%s' "$facts" | jq -r '(.author.commits_as_identity // "-") | tostring' 2>/dev/null)" || authored="-"
    fi
    [[ -n "$authored" ]] || authored="-"
    if [[ "$authored" == "-" ]] && (( ${#cmd[@]} )); then
        ## An identity whose count cannot be read is not trusted -- say so, else a broken facts
        ## command looks exactly like a contributor who is below the threshold.
        warn "'${cmd[*]}' answered no identity-authored commits for '$nid'"
    fi
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_AUTHORED[$key]="$authored"
    RAD_METADATA_AUTHORED_VALUE="$authored"
    return 0
}

## The trust table of one 'rad_comment_metadata' run.  'Who is trusted' is one question with one
## answer: the forward read and the '--last' walk both have to ask it, and a caller must not be able
## to get a different verdict out of the two paths.  Set up once per run, read by 'rad_metadata_trust'.
declare -gA RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_DELEGATES=()
declare -gA RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_NAMED=()
declare -gA RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNED=()
declare -ga RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_THRESHOLDS=()
declare -g RAD_METADATA_TRUST=""

function rad_metadata_trust ## <rid> <nid> - the verdict for one identity, in 'RAD_METADATA_TRUST'
{
    ## Empty when the identity is not trusted: a delegate, an identity named with '--trust did:…',
    ## one of the item's assignees, or an identity whose authored commits reach one of the
    ## 'contributor(<n>)' thresholds.  An identity which is not of the radicle system at all ('z6M…')
    ## is never trusted -- there is nothing to authenticate such an op against.
    local rid="$1" mdid="$2" authored n
    RAD_METADATA_TRUST=""
    [[ "$mdid" == z6M* ]] || return 0
    if [[ -n "${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_DELEGATES[$mdid]:-}" ]]; then
        RAD_METADATA_TRUST=delegate
    elif [[ -n "${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_NAMED[$mdid]:-}" ]]; then
        RAD_METADATA_TRUST=named
    elif [[ -n "${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNED[$mdid]:-}" ]]; then
        RAD_METADATA_TRUST=assignee
    else
        rad_metadata_authored "$rid" "$mdid"
        authored="$RAD_METADATA_AUTHORED_VALUE"
        if [[ "$authored" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
            for n in ${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_THRESHOLDS[@]+"${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_THRESHOLDS[@]}"}; do
                if (( authored >= n )); then
                    RAD_METADATA_TRUST="contributor($n)"
                    break
                fi
            done
        fi
    fi
    return 0
}

function rad_metadata_body ## <storage> <op> <type> <subject> - the body of one op of that COB
{
    ## Only the op kinds which can carry a body are opened ('Comment', 'Edit comment', 'Create
    ## issue', 'Edit root'), and the payload is the blob 'rad' names '0'.  An op of another COB can
    ## stand in this chain (rad chains a COB onto the identity commits), so the op has to declare the
    ## type the ref carries -- else a foreign comment would be reported against this item.
    ##
    ## Returns 1 when this op carries no body of that type; the caller skips it either way.
    local storage="$1" op="$2" type="$3" subject="$4"
    local body manifest optype
    case "$subject" in
    Comment | 'Edit comment' | 'Create issue' | 'Edit root') ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
    esac
    body="$(git --git-dir="$storage" show "$op:0" 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.body // empty' 2>/dev/null)" || body=""
    [[ -n "$body" ]] || return 1
    manifest="$(git --git-dir="$storage" show "$op:manifest" 2>/dev/null)" || manifest=""
    if [[ -n "$manifest" ]]; then
        optype="$(printf '%s' "$manifest" | jq -r '.typeName // empty' 2>/dev/null)" || optype=""
        if [[ -n "$optype" && "$optype" != "xyz.radicle.$type" ]]; then
            return 1
        fi
    fi
    printf '%s' "$body"
}

function rad_comment_metadata_ref ## <storage> <item> - the one COB the item names
{
    ## The ref listing is a single call and it is what says which COB the id names.  A COB lives in
    ## every namespace which replicated it, so it is keyed by type and id and only the newest tip is
    ## kept -- else every op would be read once per copy.
    ##
    ## Sets RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_REF/_TYPE/_ID.  Returns 3 when the node holds no such issue or
    ## patch, 2 when the id matches more than one.
    local storage="$1" item="$2"
    local epoch ref type id key
    local -A newest_ref=()
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_REF=""
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_TYPE=""
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ID=""
    while IFS="$RAD_RS" read -r epoch ref; do
        [[ -n "$ref" ]] || continue
        type="${ref#*refs/cobs/xyz.radicle.}"; type="${type%%/*}"
        id="${ref##*/}"
        [[ "$id" == "$item"* ]] || continue
        key="$type:$id"
        [[ -n "${newest_ref[$key]:-}" ]] && continue
        newest_ref[$key]="$ref"
    done < <(git --git-dir="$storage" for-each-ref --sort=-committerdate \
        --format="%(committerdate:unix)${RAD_RS}%(refname)" \
        'refs/namespaces/*/refs/cobs/xyz.radicle.issue/*' \
        'refs/namespaces/*/refs/cobs/xyz.radicle.patch/*' 2>/dev/null)

    (( ${#newest_ref[@]} )) || return 3
    (( ${#newest_ref[@]} == 1 )) || return 2      ## an issue and a patch share the prefix

    for key in "${!newest_ref[@]}"; do
        RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_REF="${newest_ref[$key]}"
        RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_TYPE="${key%%:*}"
        RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ID="${key#*:}"
    done
    return 0
}

function rad_comment_metadata_assignees ## <storage> <ref> - the item's current assignees
{
    ## The newest 'assign' op of the chain carries the whole set (a radicle op is the new value of
    ## its field), so the walk stops at the first one -- walking backwards from the tip is what makes
    ## that one read, not one read per op.  Only identities of the radicle system count here: an
    ## assignee which is not a DID/NID is dropped like any other entry without identity.
    local storage="$1" ref="$2" op payload did
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNEES=()
    while IFS= read -r op; do
        [[ -n "$op" ]] || continue
        payload="$(git --git-dir="$storage" show "$op:0" 2>/dev/null)" || continue
        [[ "$payload" == *'"type":"assign"'* ]] || continue
        while IFS= read -r did; do
            did="$(rad_metadata_nid "$did")"
            [[ "$did" == z6M* ]] || continue
            RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNEES+=( "$did" )
        done < <(printf '%s' "$payload" | jq -r '.assignees[]? // empty' 2>/dev/null)
        break
    done < <(git --git-dir="$storage" log --format=%H "$ref" 2>/dev/null)
    return 0
}

function rad_comment_metadata_collect ## <storage> <ref> <type> <id> - the marker lines of one item
{
    ## Reads the chain of the one item the ref names -- never the whole repository: the ops and their
    ## payloads are opened for that COB alone ('rad_comment_metadata_ref' is what resolved which one
    ## it is).  Answers oldest first, and inside one second in chain order: the records are laid down
    ## in the order the chain is walked and the sort keeps that order (see below).
    local storage="$1" ref="$2" type="$3" id="$4"
    local op mail iso epoch subject body key value
    local -A newest_ref=()

    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA=()
    while IFS="$RAD_RS" read -r op mail iso epoch subject; do
        body="$(rad_metadata_body "$storage" "$op" "$type" "$subject")" || continue
        while IFS="$RAD_US" read -r key value; do
            [[ -n "$key" && -n "$value" ]] || continue
            RAD_COMMENT_METADATA+=( "$epoch${RAD_US}$iso${RAD_US}$type${RAD_US}${id:0:7}${RAD_US}$op${RAD_US}${mail%%@*}${RAD_US}$(rad_metadata_mail_nid "$mail")${RAD_US}$key${RAD_US}$value" )
        done < <(rad_metadata_marks "$body")
    done < <(git --git-dir="$storage" log --reverse --topo-order \
        --format="%H${RAD_RS}%ae${RAD_RS}%aI${RAD_RS}%at${RAD_RS}%s" "$ref" 2>/dev/null)

    ## Chronological, and inside one second in *chain order*: the records were laid down as the chain
    ## was walked (parent first), so a stable sort on the epoch keeps two markers written in the same
    ## second in the order they were written.  The op sha is deliberately *not* a tie break -- it
    ## would order a pair by hash, and '--last' would point at the wrong one of the two.
    if (( ${#RAD_COMMENT_METADATA[@]} > 1 )); then
        local sorted
        sorted="$(printf '%s\n' "${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA[@]}" |
            LC_ALL=C sort -s -t"$RAD_US" -k1,1n)" || sorted=""
        RAD_COMMENT_METADATA=()
        while IFS= read -r line; do
            [[ -n "$line" ]] && RAD_COMMENT_METADATA+=( "$line" )
        done <<<"$sorted"
    fi
}

function rad_comment_metadata_newest ## <storage> <rid> <ref> <type> <id> <key>.. - the newest block per key
{
    ## The '--last' walk: newest op first, and it stops as soon as every requested key has its newest
    ## *trusted* entry -- so the common case opens one payload instead of the whole chain.  The walk
    ## skips a marker whose author is not attributable, and that is not a stopper: an older trusted
    ## block still applies, else one comment by a passer-by would freeze an item's state.
    ##
    ## 'The newest block carrying that key, taken whole' is meant per key: every line of a key it
    ## found is returned (a comment proposing two labels is one application), and a key nobody asked
    ## for is left alone.  No <key> at all asks for *every* key, which is what the forward read
    ## answers; then the walk cannot stop early, because a key may first appear anywhere in the chain.
    ##
    ## Answers in the shape the forward read answers in -- oldest first, one
    ## '<epoch>US<iso>US<type>US<id>US<op>US<alias>US<did>US<trust>US<key>US<value>' per line.
    local storage="$1" rid="$2" ref="$3" type="$4" id="$5"
    shift 5
    local -a wanted=( "$@" ) chain=() order=() lines=()
    local all=""
    local -A is_wanted=() have=() chosen=() answered=()
    local -i left=${#wanted[@]} n i
    local key want line op mail iso epoch subject body mdid trust

    if (( ${#wanted[@]} == 0 )); then
        all=1
    else
        for key in "${wanted[@]}"; do
            is_wanted["$key"]=1
        done
    fi

    while IFS="$RAD_RS" read -r op mail iso epoch subject; do
        [[ -n "$op" ]] || continue
        chain+=( "$op${RAD_US}$mail${RAD_US}$iso${RAD_US}$epoch${RAD_US}$subject" )
    done < <(git --git-dir="$storage" log --reverse --topo-order \
        --format="%H${RAD_RS}%ae${RAD_RS}%aI${RAD_RS}%at${RAD_RS}%s" "$ref" 2>/dev/null)

    ## Walked in the order the forward read *ends* in -- newest epoch first, and inside one second the
    ## chain's last position -- so 'the newest block' is the last record of that answer and not merely
    ## the tip of the chain.  A chain whose dates are not monotone (an op replayed with an older
    ## clock) would otherwise let the two paths point at different records.
    while IFS= read -r n; do
        [[ -n "$n" ]] && order+=( "$n" )
    done < <(
        for (( i = 0; i < ${#chain[@]}; i++ )); do
            IFS="$RAD_US" read -r _ _ _ epoch _ <<<"${chain[i]}"
            printf '%s%s%s\n' "$epoch" "$RAD_US" "$i"
        done | LC_ALL=C sort -t"$RAD_US" -k1,1nr -k2,2nr | cut -d"$RAD_US" -f2
    )

    for n in ${order[@]+"${order[@]}"}; do
        (( left > 0 )) || [[ -n "$all" ]] || break
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r op mail iso epoch subject <<<"${chain[n]}"
        body="$(rad_metadata_body "$storage" "$op" "$type" "$subject")" || continue
        mdid="$(rad_metadata_mail_nid "$mail")"
        rad_metadata_trust "$rid" "$mdid"
        trust="$RAD_METADATA_TRUST"
        [[ -n "$trust" ]] || continue
        ## The markers of this op.  A key nobody has answered yet is answered by this op -- with
        ## every line of the key it carries, so a comment proposing two labels is one application --
        ## and a key a *newer* op already answered is not answered again here.
        lines=() answered=()
        while IFS="$RAD_US" read -r key want; do
            [[ -n "$key" && -n "$want" ]] || continue
            [[ -z "$all" && -z "${is_wanted[$key]:-}" ]] && continue
            lines+=( "$key${RAD_US}$want" )
            [[ -n "${have[$key]:-}" ]] && continue
            have["$key"]=1
            answered["$key"]=1
            [[ -z "${is_wanted[$key]:-}" ]] || left=$(( left - 1 ))
        done < <(rad_metadata_marks "$body")
        (( ${#answered[@]} )) || continue
        for line in ${lines[@]+"${lines[@]}"}; do
            key="${line%%"$RAD_US"*}"
            [[ -n "${answered[$key]:-}" ]] || continue
            chosen["$n"]+="$epoch${RAD_US}$iso${RAD_US}$type${RAD_US}${id:0:7}${RAD_US}$op${RAD_US}${mail%%@*}${RAD_US}$mdid${RAD_US}$trust${RAD_US}$key${RAD_US}${line#*"$RAD_US"}"$'\n'
        done
    done

    ## Oldest first, like the forward read: the chain position decides the order, never the date --
    ## an add and its undo land in the same second routinely (the walk above is newest first).
    for (( i = ${#order[@]} - 1; i >= 0; i-- )); do
        n="${order[i]}"
        printf '%s' "${chosen["$n"]:-}"
    done
    return 0
}

function rad_comment_metadata_json ## - the whole answer as one object
{
    ## Complete, never elided: the entries the filter kept, oldest first.
    local entry epoch iso type id op alias did trust key value first=""
    local -a entries=()
    if (( ${#RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_TRUSTED[@]} )); then
        while IFS="$RAD_US" read -r epoch iso type id op alias did trust key value; do
            [[ -n "$value" ]] || continue
            entries+=( "{\"item\":$(json_string "$type"),\"key\":$(json_string "$key"),\"id\":$(json_string "$id"),\"op\":$(json_string "$op"),\"author\":$(json_string "$alias"),\"did\":$(json_string "did:key:$did"),\"trust\":$(json_string "$trust"),\"date\":$(json_string "$iso"),\"value\":$(json_string "$value")}" )
        done <<<"$(printf '%s\n' "${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_TRUSTED[@]}")"
    fi
    for entry in "${entries[@]}"; do
        first+="${first:+,}$entry"
    done
    ## The assignees belong into the answer: they are one of the reasons an entry is trusted, and a
    ## caller which sees a surprise wants to know where the trust came from.  Identities travel in
    ## the form the radicle tools want ('rad issue assign --add <DID>'), the same one the text
    ## output prints: a consumer must never have to re-normalize them.
    local assignees=""
    for did in ${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNEES[@]+"${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNEES[@]}"}; do
        assignees+="${assignees:+,}$(json_string "did:key:$did")"
    done
    printf '{"bar_rad":1,"query":%s,"repo":%s,"count":%s,"assignees":[%s],"entries":[%s]}\n' \
        "$(json_string "$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_QUERY")" "$(json_string "$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_RID")" \
        "$(json_number "${#entries[@]}")" "$assignees" "$first"
}

function rad_comment_metadata_usage ## [reason] - usage on stderr
{
    [[ -n "${1:-}" ]] && printf 'rad_comment_metadata: %s\n' "$1" >&2
    printf 'usage: rad_comment_metadata <item> [--query <key>].. [--repo <rid|path>] [--trust <spec>].. [--last] [--json]\n' >&2
    printf '       <item> the issue or patch (short or full id) the question is about\n' >&2
    printf '       <key>  one proposal key to answer for, repeatable; no key at all answers for every\n' >&2
    printf '              key a comment proposes.  The keys with an effect are label, assign, unassign\n' >&2
    printf '       <spec> one of did:<did|nid>, contributor(<n>); the delegates and the identities\n' >&2
    printf '              the item is assigned to are trusted without a spec\n' >&2
    printf '       --last the newest block per key, which is what applies it\n' >&2
}

## <name> <rid|path> - the repository and the node storage of it, in the 'RAD_METADATA_*' variables
function rad_metadata_store
{
    ## Which repository: an explicit one (RID, or a path entered to ask that checkout), else the one
    ## this runs in.  Sets 'RAD_METADATA_RID' and 'RAD_METADATA_STORAGE'.  '<name>' is the caller,
    ## only for its diagnostics -- 'not a repository here' and 'the node holds no storage' are not
    ## the same answer for the operator, and neither is a usage error.
    local name="$1" repo="$2"
    local toplevel="" rid="" radpath="" storage=""
    if [[ -n "$repo" && "$repo" != rad:* ]]; then
        if ! toplevel="$(cd "$repo" 2>/dev/null && git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)"; then
            error "not a git repository: '$repo' is neither a RID nor a checkout"
            return 3
        fi
    elif [[ -z "$repo" ]]; then
        if ! toplevel="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)"; then
            error "not a git repository: 'bar $name' reads the checkout it runs in"
            return 3
        fi
    fi
    if [[ -n "$toplevel" ]]; then
        rid="$(cd "$toplevel" && rad_run inspect --rid)" || return $?
        rid="${rid##*$'\n'}"
    else
        rid="$repo"
    fi
    radpath="$(rad_path)" || radpath=""
    storage="$radpath/storage/${rid#rad:}"
    if [[ -z "$radpath" || ! -d "$storage" ]]; then
        error "the node holds no storage for '$rid': '${RAD_BIN} path' says '${radpath:--}'"
        return 3
    fi
    RAD_METADATA_RID="$rid"
    RAD_METADATA_STORAGE="$storage"
    return 0
}

function rad_comment_metadata ## see the module header
{
    local -a args=() specs=() parts=() queries=()
    local raw arg value json="" repo="" last="" item="" item_rc=0
    local toplevel="" rid="" radpath="" storage="" delegates="" line did spec key
    local epoch iso type id op alias mdid trust n
    local -a trusted=()
    local -i i=0

    for raw in "$@"; do
        if [[ "$raw" == --*=* ]]; then
            args+=("${raw%%=*}" "${raw#*=}")
        else
            args+=("$raw")
        fi
    done
    local -i count=${#args[@]}
    i=0
    while (( i < count )); do
        arg="${args[i]}"
        i=$(( i + 1 ))
        case "$arg" in
        -h | --help)
            rad_comment_metadata_usage 2>&1
            return 0
            ;;
        --json) json=1 ;;
        --last) last=1 ;;
        --query | --repo | --trust)
            if (( i >= count )) || [[ -z "${args[i]}" ]]; then
                rad_comment_metadata_usage "'$arg' needs a non-empty value"
                return 2
            fi
            value="${args[i]}"
            i=$(( i + 1 ))
            case "$arg" in
            --query)
                if [[ ! "$value" =~ ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$ ]]; then
                    rad_comment_metadata_usage "invalid --query '$value': a key is a word of letters, digits, '_' and '-'"
                    return 2
                fi
                queries+=( "$value" )
                ;;
            --repo) repo="$value" ;;
            --trust)
                local part
                IFS=',' read -r -a parts <<<"$value"
                for part in "${parts[@]}"; do
                    [[ -n "$part" ]] && specs+=( "$part" )
                done
                ;;
            esac
            ;;
        -*)
            rad_comment_metadata_usage "unknown argument '$arg'"
            return 2
            ;;
        *)
            ## the one item the question is about (the only positional argument there is)
            if [[ -n "$item" ]]; then
                rad_comment_metadata_usage "only one item can be asked about (got '$item' and '$arg')"
                return 2
            fi
            item="$arg"
            ;;
        esac
    done

    [[ -n "${BAR_AGENT_JSON:-}" ]] && json=1
    if [[ -z "$item" ]]; then
        rad_comment_metadata_usage "the item is required: an issue or patch id, short or full"
        return 2
    fi
    if ! command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        printf 'rad_comment_metadata: jq is required to read the op payloads\n' >&2
        return 2
    fi

    ## The trust specs are validated before anything is read: a usage error is not worth a node
    ## lookup, and it has to be reported the same way whether or not the node holds the repository.
    for spec in "${specs[@]}"; do
        case "$spec" in
        did:*) ;;
        'contributor('*')')
            n="${spec#'contributor('}"; n="${n%')'}"
            if [[ ! "$n" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
                rad_comment_metadata_usage "invalid trust spec 'contributor($n)', want a number"
                return 2
            fi
            ;;
        *)
            rad_comment_metadata_usage "unknown trust spec '$spec', want did:<did|nid> or contributor(<n>)"
            return 2
            ;;
        esac
    done

    ## 'rad' first: without it nothing can be resolved, and "the node holds no storage" would be
    ## the wrong answer for the operator to chase.
    is_command_installed "$RAD_BIN" || {
        error "'$RAD_BIN' is not installed"
        return 1
    }

    ## Which repository: an explicit one (RID or path), else the checkout this runs in.
    rad_metadata_store rad_comment_metadata "$repo" || return $?
    rid="$RAD_METADATA_RID"
    storage="$RAD_METADATA_STORAGE"

    ## Who is trusted: the delegates answer from the identity document, 'did:' names one identity and
    ## 'contributor(<n>)' is decided from the authored commits the fact tool counts.  The table is
    ## module state, because the '--last' walk asks the same question from its own loop.
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_DELEGATES=()
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_NAMED=()
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNED=()
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_THRESHOLDS=()
    delegates="$(rad_run inspect "$rid" --delegates)" || return $?
    while IFS= read -r line; do
        did="${line%% *}"
        [[ -n "$did" ]] || continue
        RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_DELEGATES["$(rad_metadata_nid "$did")"]=1
    done <<<"$delegates"

    for spec in "${specs[@]}"; do
        case "$spec" in
        did:*) RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_NAMED["$(rad_metadata_nid "$spec")"]=1 ;;
        'contributor('*')')
            n="${spec#'contributor('}"; n="${n%')'}"
            RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_THRESHOLDS+=( "$n" )
            ;;
        esac
    done

    ## An asked-for contributor threshold needs the facts command: without it every count reads
    ## 'unknown', which would answer 'nothing is trusted' where the operator asked for trust.
    if (( ${#RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_THRESHOLDS[@]} )) && [[ -z "${RAD_TRUST_CMD:-}" ]]; then
        rad_comment_metadata_usage "'--trust contributor(<n>)' needs RAD_TRUST_CMD: the command which answers the authored commits"
        return 2
    fi

    rad_comment_metadata_ref "$storage" "$item"
    item_rc=$?
    if (( item_rc != 0 )); then
        case "$item_rc" in
        2)
            rad_comment_metadata_usage "'$item' matches more than one issue or patch, use more characters"
            return 2
            ;;
        *)
            error "the node holds no issue or patch '$item' in '$rid'"
            return 3
            ;;
        esac
    fi
    rad_comment_metadata_assignees "$storage" "$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_REF"

    ## The item's assignees are a trust source of their own: assigning an issue to a contributor
    ## makes that contributor's proposals count -- so the same query can answer differently before
    ## and after an assignment.  That is the design: trust is read when it is asked for, never
    ## stored, and it is always an identity of the radicle system ('z6M…') which carries it.
    for did in ${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNEES[@]+"${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNEES[@]}"}; do
        RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNED["$did"]=1
    done

    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_QUERY="${queries[*]}"
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_RID="$rid"
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_TRUSTED=()
    if [[ -n "$last" ]]; then
        ## '--last' is the newest block *per key* and it is answered from the tip of the chain
        ## backwards: what applies is known without reading what it superseded.
        while IFS="$RAD_US" read -r epoch iso type id op alias mdid trust key value; do
            [[ -n "$value" ]] || continue
            trusted+=( "$epoch${RAD_US}$iso${RAD_US}$type${RAD_US}$id${RAD_US}$op${RAD_US}$alias${RAD_US}$mdid${RAD_US}$trust${RAD_US}$key${RAD_US}$value" )
        done < <(rad_comment_metadata_newest "$storage" "$rid" "$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_REF" \
            "$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_TYPE" "$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ID" ${queries[@]+"${queries[@]}"})
    else
        rad_comment_metadata_collect "$storage" "$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_REF" \
            "$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_TYPE" "$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ID"
        while IFS="$RAD_US" read -r epoch iso type id op alias mdid key value; do
            [[ -n "$value" ]] || continue
            (( ${#queries[@]} == 0 )) || [[ " ${queries[*]} " == *" $key "* ]] || continue
            ## No radicle identity in the op's author (a git-only author, or a mail which is not
            ## '<alias>@<nid>'): there is nothing to authenticate against, so the proposal is dropped
            ## -- silently, like every other proposal which is not trusted.
            rad_metadata_trust "$rid" "$mdid"
            trust="$RAD_METADATA_TRUST"
            [[ -n "$trust" ]] || continue
            trusted+=( "$epoch${RAD_US}$iso${RAD_US}$type${RAD_US}$id${RAD_US}$op${RAD_US}$alias${RAD_US}$mdid${RAD_US}$trust${RAD_US}$key${RAD_US}$value" )
        done <<<"$(printf '%s\n' "${RAD_COMMENT_METADATA[@]:-}")"
    fi
    RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_TRUSTED=( ${trusted[@]+"${trusted[@]}"} )

    if [[ -n "$json" ]]; then
        rad_comment_metadata_json
        return 0
    fi
    (( ${#trusted[@]} )) || return 0      ## nothing trusted is an answer, not a failure
    for line in "${trusted[@]}"; do
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r epoch iso type id op alias mdid trust key value <<<"$line"
        printf '%s item=%s:%s author=%s did=did:key:%s trust=%s date=%s value=%s\n' \
            "$key" "$type" "$id" "$alias" "$mdid" "$trust" "$iso" "$value"
    done
}

# --- the write layer: one function per effect, each one 'rad' invocation -----------------------

function rad_metadata_rad ## <repo> <args>.. - one 'rad' invocation, in the repository the caller means
{
    ## Every applier below does exactly one invocation of 'rad' and this is where it runs: an empty
    ## <repo> is the checkout the command runs in, a path is entered, a RID is handed to rad with
    ## '-r' (rad then needs no checkout at all).  Nothing is written anywhere else and there is no
    ## second write: an applier either makes its one call or it refuses.
    local repo="$1"
    shift
    if [[ -z "$repo" ]]; then
        rad_run "$@"
    elif [[ "$repo" == rad:* ]]; then
        rad_run "$@" -r "$repo"
    else
        ( cd "$repo" 2>/dev/null && rad_run "$@" )
    fi
}

function rad_metadata_identity ## <repo> <did|alias> - the DID the write commands take
{
    ## A comment cannot be trusted to spell a DID right, so an alias is resolved before it is written
    ## -- by the same fact command the trust gate asks ('RAD_TRUST_CMD', the tool behind 'rad_trust'),
    ## for the repository the caller names (or this checkout).  A value which is already a DID/NID
    ## needs no command; one which resolves to nothing is refused rather than written as it stands.
    local repo="$1" value="$2"
    local -a cmd=()
    local nid="" facts=""
    nid="$(rad_metadata_nid "$value")"
    if [[ "$nid" == z6M* ]]; then
        printf 'did:key:%s' "$nid"
        return 0
    fi
    read -r -a cmd <<<"${RAD_TRUST_CMD:-}"
    if (( ${#cmd[@]} )) && facts="$("${cmd[@]}" --repo "${repo:-$PWD}" --author "$value" 2>/dev/null)"; then
        nid="$(printf '%s' "$facts" | jq -r '(.author.nid // .author.did // "") | tostring' 2>/dev/null)" || nid=""
        nid="$(rad_metadata_nid "$nid")"
        if [[ "$nid" == z6M* ]]; then
            printf 'did:key:%s' "$nid"
            return 0
        fi
    fi
    error "cannot resolve '$value' to a radicle identity: it is neither a DID/NID nor an alias the repository knows"
    return 1
}

function rad_metadata_labels ## <repo> <type> <id> - the labels the item carries now, one per line
{
    ## The mutation target, read fresh and never remembered: the Labels column of 'rad <type> list'
    ## is what the item carries, and the applier knows what it has to delete from it.  This is *not*
    ## how the chain state is read -- a label written after a proposal is stale -- it is a different
    ## question ('what is there' against 'what was proposed').
    local repo="$1" type="$2" id="$3"
    local sub="issue" table rows row cell label
    [[ "$type" == patch ]] && sub="patch"
    table="$(rad_metadata_rad "$repo" "$sub" list --all)" || return 1
    rows="$(rad_state_table_checked "$sub list --all" "$table" ID Labels)" || return 1
    while IFS="$RAD_US" read -r row cell; do
        [[ -n "$cell" ]] || continue
        [[ "$row" == "$id"* ]] || continue
        local IFS=','
        for label in $cell; do
            label="${label#"${label%%[![:space:]]*}"}"
            label="${label%"${label##*[![:space:]]}"}"
            [[ -n "$label" ]] && printf '%s\n' "$label"
        done
    done <<<"$rows"
    return 0
}

## <item> - the 'rad' word and the id of an item as the reader answers it
function rad_metadata_item
{
    ## '<type>:<id>' is the form 'rad_comment_metadata' answers with, so it is the form the appliers
    ## take; a bare id is an issue, which is the common case (labels and assignments are mostly issue
    ## business, and the read side names the type anyway).
    local item="$1"
    RAD_METADATA_SUB="issue"
    RAD_METADATA_ID="$item"
    case "$item" in
    issue:*) RAD_METADATA_SUB="issue"; RAD_METADATA_ID="${item#issue:}" ;;
    patch:*) RAD_METADATA_SUB="patch"; RAD_METADATA_ID="${item#patch:}" ;;
    esac
}

function rad_set_label_usage ## [reason] - usage on stderr
{
    [[ -n "${1:-}" ]] && printf 'rad_set_label: %s\n' "$1" >&2
    printf 'usage: rad_set_label <item> <add> [<delete>].. [--repo <rid|path>]\n' >&2
    printf '       <item> issue:<id>, patch:<id> or a bare issue id\n' >&2
}

function rad_set_label ## <item> <add> [<delete>].. [--repo <rid|path>] - one 'rad <type> label'
{
    ## One label added, and any number of labels deleted in the same write: that is what makes an
    ## item carry one chain state at a time (the new one in, the previous one out).  A label the item
    ## does not carry is rad's business, not this function's -- it hands rad exactly what it was
    ## given and reports what it did.
    local -a deletes=() args=()
    local raw value item="" add="" repo=""
    for raw in "$@"; do
        if [[ "$raw" == --repo=* ]]; then
            args+=(--repo "${raw#--repo=}")
        else
            args+=("$raw")
        fi
    done
    local -i i=0 count=${#args[@]}
    while (( i < count )); do
        raw="${args[i]}"
        i=$(( i + 1 ))
        case "$raw" in
        -h | --help)
            rad_set_label_usage 2>&1
            return 0
            ;;
        --repo)
            if (( i >= count )) || [[ -z "${args[i]}" ]]; then
                rad_set_label_usage "'--repo' needs a non-empty value"
                return 2
            fi
            repo="${args[i]}"
            i=$(( i + 1 ))
            ;;
        -*)
            rad_set_label_usage "unknown argument '$raw'"
            return 2
            ;;
        *)
            if [[ -z "$item" ]]; then
                item="$raw"
            elif [[ -z "$add" ]]; then
                add="$raw"
            else
                deletes+=( "$raw" )
            fi
            ;;
        esac
    done
    if [[ -z "$item" || -z "$add" ]]; then
        rad_set_label_usage "an item and the label to add are required"
        return 2
    fi
    rad_metadata_item "$item"
    local -a call=("$RAD_METADATA_SUB" label "$RAD_METADATA_ID" --quiet "--add=$add")
    for value in ${deletes[@]+"${deletes[@]}"}; do
        call+=( "--delete=$value" )
    done
    rad_metadata_rad "$repo" "${call[@]}" || return $?
    printf 'applied label item=%s add=%s' "$item" "$add"
    (( ${#deletes[@]} )) && printf ' delete=%s' "${deletes[*]}"
    printf '\n'
}

function rad_assign_usage ## [reason] - usage on stderr
{
    [[ -n "${1:-}" ]] && printf '%s: %s\n' "$RAD_ASSIGN_WORD" "$1" >&2
    printf 'usage: %s <item> <did|alias> [--repo <rid|path>]\n' "$RAD_ASSIGN_WORD" >&2
    printf '       <item> issue:<id>, patch:<id> or a bare issue id\n' >&2
}

## <item> <did|alias> [--repo <rid|path>] - one 'rad <type> assign' add or delete
function rad_metadata_assign
{
    local word="$1" remove="$2"
    shift 2
    local -a args=()
    local raw value item="" who="" repo="" did="" arg
    RAD_ASSIGN_WORD="$word"
    for raw in "$@"; do
        if [[ "$raw" == --repo=* ]]; then
            args+=(--repo "${raw#--repo=}")
        else
            args+=("$raw")
        fi
    done
    local -i i=0 count=${#args[@]}
    while (( i < count )); do
        raw="${args[i]}"
        i=$(( i + 1 ))
        case "$raw" in
        -h | --help)
            rad_assign_usage 2>&1
            return 0
            ;;
        --repo)
            if (( i >= count )) || [[ -z "${args[i]}" ]]; then
                rad_assign_usage "'--repo' needs a non-empty value"
                return 2
            fi
            repo="${args[i]}"
            i=$(( i + 1 ))
            ;;
        -*)
            rad_assign_usage "unknown argument '$raw'"
            return 2
            ;;
        *)
            if [[ -z "$item" ]]; then
                item="$raw"
            elif [[ -z "$who" ]]; then
                who="$raw"
            else
                rad_assign_usage "unexpected argument '$raw'"
                return 2
            fi
            ;;
        esac
    done
    if [[ -z "$item" || -z "$who" ]]; then
        rad_assign_usage "an item and an identity are required"
        return 2
    fi
    rad_metadata_item "$item"
    did="$(rad_metadata_identity "$repo" "$who")" || return 1
    if [[ -n "$remove" ]]; then
        arg="--delete=$did"
    else
        arg="--add=$did"
    fi
    rad_metadata_rad "$repo" "$RAD_METADATA_SUB" assign "$RAD_METADATA_ID" --quiet "$arg" || return $?
    if [[ -n "$remove" ]]; then
        printf 'applied unassign item=%s delete=%s\n' "$item" "$did"
    else
        printf 'applied assign item=%s add=%s\n' "$item" "$did"
    fi
}

function rad_assign ## <item> <did|alias> [--repo <rid|path>] - one 'rad <type> assign --add'
{
    rad_metadata_assign rad_assign "" "$@"
}

function rad_unassign ## <item> <did|alias> [--repo <rid|path>] - one 'rad <type> assign --delete'
{
    rad_metadata_assign rad_unassign 1 "$@"
}

function rad_apply_labels ## <repo> <dry> <entry>.. - the label state of one item, reconciled and applied
{
    ## The label entries of one item are one *state*: the labels the chain proposes against the
    ## labels the item carries, which are read fresh (nothing is remembered between calls).  The
    ## first write of the item carries the labels the proposal does not carry -- the previous chain
    ## state -- so the item ends with the state the trusted block asks for, and a label the item
    ## already says is not written again.
    ##
    ## An <entry> is one line of the reader's answer as 'key<TAB>item<TAB>id<TAB>value'.  Entries
    ## which are not labels are not this function's business.  A stored label no proposal asks for is
    ## reported and left alone: deleting state nobody proposed is not what the proposals asked for.
    local repo="$1" dry="$2"
    shift 2
    local -a entries=( "$@" ) derived=() adds=() deletes=() repopt=() call=()
    local entry key type id value label itemref="" stored="" rc=0
    local -i i=0

    for entry in ${entries[@]+"${entries[@]}"}; do
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r key type id value <<<"$entry"
        [[ "$key" == label ]] || continue
        itemref="$type:$id"
        break
    done
    [[ -n "$itemref" ]] || return 0
    [[ -n "$repo" ]] && repopt=( --repo "$repo" )
    type="${itemref%%:*}"
    id="${itemref#*:}"

    ## What the chain proposes (every label of the item's block) and what the item carries now.
    for entry in ${entries[@]+"${entries[@]}"}; do
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r key type id value <<<"$entry"
        [[ "$key" == label && "$type:$id" == "$itemref" ]] || continue
        derived+=( "$value" )
    done
    type="${itemref%%:*}"
    id="${itemref#*:}"
    stored="$(rad_metadata_labels "$repo" "$type" "$id")" || rc=1
    while IFS= read -r label; do
        [[ -n "$label" ]] || continue
        [[ " ${derived[*]} " == *" $label "* ]] && continue
        deletes+=( "$label" )
    done <<<"$stored"

    ## What to add, in the order the block wrote it, and what is already said.
    for entry in ${entries[@]+"${entries[@]}"}; do
        IFS="$RAD_US" read -r key type id value <<<"$entry"
        [[ "$key" == label && "$type:$id" == "$itemref" ]] || continue
        if [[ $'\n'"$stored"$'\n' == *$'\n'"$value"$'\n'* ]]; then
            printf 'skip label item=%s add=%s (the item already says it)\n' "$itemref" "$value"
            continue
        fi
        adds+=( "$value" )
    done

    if (( ${#adds[@]} == 0 )); then
        if (( ${#deletes[@]} )); then
            printf 'skip label item=%s (%s is carried by the item, no proposal asks for it)\n' \
                "$itemref" "${deletes[*]}"
        fi
        return $rc
    fi
    for i in "${!adds[@]}"; do
        if [[ -n "$dry" ]]; then
            printf 'would label item=%s add=%s' "$itemref" "${adds[i]}"
            (( i == 0 && ${#deletes[@]} )) && printf ' delete=%s' "${deletes[*]}"
            printf '\n'
            continue
        fi
        call=( "$itemref" "${adds[i]}" )
        (( i == 0 )) && call+=( ${deletes[@]+"${deletes[@]}"} )
        call+=( ${repopt[@]+"${repopt[@]}"} )
        rad_set_label "${call[@]}" || rc=1
    done
    return $rc
}

function rad_apply_from_comments_usage ## [reason] - usage on stderr
{
    [[ -n "${1:-}" ]] && printf 'rad_apply_from_comments: %s\n' "$1" >&2
    printf 'usage: rad_apply_from_comments <item>.. [--query <key>].. [--repo <rid|path>]\n' >&2
    printf '       [--trust <spec>].. [--dry-run]\n' >&2
    printf '       <item> an issue or patch (short or full id) whose proposals are applied\n' >&2
    printf '       The newest trusted block per key is what applies; superseded ones are history.\n' >&2
}

function rad_apply_from_comments ## see the module header
{
    ## The maintenance side of the reader: it asks 'rad_comment_metadata' for the trusted proposals
    ## of an item ('--last', which is the state; a block a later one superseded is history) and
    ## dispatches every entry to the applier of its key.  The reader owns the trust decision -- an
    ## entry whose author cannot be attributed never reaches this function, and it is not reported
    ## here because it was never an entry -- and this function owns the effect.  The keys it knows
    ## are 'label', 'assign' and 'unassign'; any other key is reported and never guessed at.
    local -a args=() items=() entries=() queries=() specs=() parts=() repopt=() call=()
    local raw value json="" repo="" dry="" item="" entry="" key="" type="" id="" itemref="" did=""
    local rc=0 failed=0
    local -i i=0 count=0

    for raw in "$@"; do
        if [[ "$raw" == --*=* ]]; then
            args+=("${raw%%=*}" "${raw#*=}")
        else
            args+=("$raw")
        fi
    done
    count=${#args[@]}
    while (( i < count )); do
        raw="${args[i]}"
        i=$(( i + 1 ))
        case "$raw" in
        -h | --help)
            rad_apply_from_comments_usage 2>&1
            return 0
            ;;
        --dry-run) dry=1 ;;
        --query | --repo | --trust)
            if (( i >= count )) || [[ -z "${args[i]}" ]]; then
                rad_apply_from_comments_usage "'$raw' needs a non-empty value"
                return 2
            fi
            value="${args[i]}"
            i=$(( i + 1 ))
            case "$raw" in
            --query) queries+=( --query "$value" ) ;;
            --repo) repo="$value" ;;
            --trust)
                IFS=',' read -r -a parts <<<"$value"
                for value in ${parts[@]+"${parts[@]}"}; do
                    [[ -n "$value" ]] && specs+=( --trust "$value" )
                done
                ;;
            esac
            ;;
        -*)
            rad_apply_from_comments_usage "unknown argument '$raw'"
            return 2
            ;;
        *) items+=( "$raw" ) ;;
        esac
    done
    if (( ${#items[@]} == 0 )); then
        rad_apply_from_comments_usage "at least one item is required"
        return 2
    fi
    if ! command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        printf 'rad_apply_from_comments: jq is required to read the entries of the reader\n' >&2
        return 2
    fi
    [[ -n "$repo" ]] && repopt=( --repo "$repo" )

    for item in "${items[@]}"; do
        call=( "$item" --last --json )
        call+=( ${queries[@]+"${queries[@]}"} )
        call+=( ${specs[@]+"${specs[@]}"} )
        call+=( ${repopt[@]+"${repopt[@]}"} )
        if ! json="$(rad_comment_metadata "${call[@]}")"; then
            rc=$?
            printf 'rad_apply_from_comments: no answer for %s\n' "$item" >&2
            failed=1
            continue
        fi
        entries=()
        while IFS= read -r entry; do
            [[ -n "$entry" ]] && entries+=( "$entry" )
        done < <(printf '%s' "$json" | jq -r '.entries[] | [.key,.item,.id,.value] | @tsv' | tr '\t' "$RAD_US")
        ## The labels first: they are one state of the item, and the write which changes it may have
        ## to delete the state it replaces.  Then everything else in the order it was written.
        rad_apply_labels "$repo" "$dry" ${entries[@]+"${entries[@]}"} || failed=1
        for entry in ${entries[@]+"${entries[@]}"}; do
            IFS="$RAD_US" read -r key type id value <<<"$entry"
            [[ "$key" == label ]] && continue
            itemref="$type:$id"
            case "$key" in
            assign | unassign)
                if ! did="$(rad_metadata_identity "$repo" "$value")"; then
                    printf 'skip %s item=%s value=%s (no identity behind it)\n' "$key" "$itemref" "$value"
                    failed=1
                    continue
                fi
                if [[ -n "$dry" ]]; then
                    if [[ "$key" == assign ]]; then
                        printf 'would assign item=%s add=%s\n' "$itemref" "$did"
                    else
                        printf 'would unassign item=%s delete=%s\n' "$itemref" "$did"
                    fi
                    continue
                fi
                if [[ "$key" == assign ]]; then
                    rad_assign "$itemref" "$value" ${repopt[@]+"${repopt[@]}"} || failed=1
                else
                    rad_unassign "$itemref" "$value" ${repopt[@]+"${repopt[@]}"} || failed=1
                fi
                ;;
            *)
                printf 'skip %s item=%s value=%s (no effect is registered for that key)\n' \
                    "$key" "$itemref" "$value"
                ;;
            esac
        done
    done
    return $(( rc | failed ))
}

# --- the reaction API: compare and swap on the caller's own reaction state ---------------------
#
# Reactions are *private per identity* and never delegated: the marker an identity leaves is read
# back by reducing the ops that identity wrote, which is why this needs none of the reader's trust
# plumbing.  rad offers no way to set a reaction to a state, only to add one or (at patch level) to
# take one back, so a transition is expressed as 'the marker I expect' -> 'the marker I want' and the
# expectation is checked against the state the store reduces before anything is written.

function rad_reaction_accepts ## <char> - 0 when the store would accept it as a reaction
{
    ## One codepoint of rad's allow list (heartwood, 'crates/radicle/src/cob/common.rs').  An emoji
    ## picker hands out '❤️' (heart plus a variation selector) and other sequences, and the store
    ## refuses those with 'invalid reaction': refusing them here costs no call and no write, and it is
    ## the same list -- a character this accepts and the store does not would be a bad promise.
    local char="$1" cp range
    ## U+1F600..U+1F64F, U+1FA75..U+1FA77, U+1FAC0..U+1FAC6, U+1FAE0..U+1FAF8,
    ## U+1F300..U+1F5FF, U+2700..U+27BF, U+1F900..U+1F9FF, U+1F680..U+1F6FF
    local -a ranges=(
        128512:128591 129653:129655 129728:129734 129760:129784
        127744:128511 9984:10175 129280:129535 128640:128767
    )
    [[ "${#char}" == 1 ]] || return 1
    cp="$(printf '%d' "'$char" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
    [[ "$cp" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 1
    for range in "${ranges[@]}"; do
        (( cp >= ${range%%:*} && cp <= ${range##*:} )) && return 0
    done
    return 1
}

function rad_metadata_reactions ## <storage> <ref> <kind> <target> <nid> - our markers on one target
{
    ## The reaction state of one target as the store reduces it, for *our* identity alone: the newest
    ## op per (target, emoji) whose author mail carries our NID, with 'active' read off that survivor.
    ## A withdrawal supersedes the add it withdraws without deleting it, so reading 'active' off the
    ## op stream would keep reporting markers their author took back.  The order is the op's position
    ## in the chain (parent before child), never the op sha: an add and its undo land in the same
    ## second routinely.
    ##
    ## <kind> says which reaction of which COB is meant: 'comment' matches the op's 'id' (an issue's
    ## root comment carries the issue id, a comment its own), 'revision' matches 'revision' (the
    ## patch id is its root revision's id) and 'revision-comment' matches 'comment'.  <target> may be
    ## short: the payload carries the full id.  Prints '<position>US<emoji>US<active>US<op>' per
    ## survivor, oldest first.
    local storage="$1" ref="$2" kind="$3" target="$4" nid="$5"
    local op mail subject payload type id revision comment emoji active candidate=""
    local -A survivor=()
    local -i pos=0
    while IFS="$RAD_RS" read -r op mail subject; do
        [[ -n "$op" ]] || continue
        case "$subject" in
        React | 'React comment') ;;
        *) continue ;;
        esac
        [[ "$(rad_metadata_mail_nid "$mail")" == "$nid" ]] || continue
        payload="$(git --git-dir="$storage" show "$op:0" 2>/dev/null)" || continue
        ## The fields over 'RAD_US', not over a tab: a tab is *whitespace* to 'read' and empty fields
        ## would collapse, which would shift every field behind them.
        {
            IFS="$RAD_US" read -r type id revision comment emoji active
        } < <(printf '%s' "$payload" |
            jq -r '[.type // "", .id // "", .revision // "", .comment // "", (.reaction // ""), ((.active // false) | tostring)] | @tsv' 2>/dev/null |
            tr '\t' "$RAD_US")
        candidate=""
        case "$kind" in
        comment)
            [[ "$type" == comment.react ]] && candidate="$id"
            ;;
        revision)
            [[ "$type" == revision.react ]] && candidate="$revision"
            ;;
        revision-comment)
            [[ "$type" == revision.comment.react ]] && candidate="$comment"
            ;;
        esac
        [[ -n "$candidate" && "$candidate" == "$target"* ]] || continue
        survivor["$emoji"]="$pos${RAD_US}$active${RAD_US}$op"
        pos=$(( pos + 1 ))
    done < <(git --git-dir="$storage" log --reverse --topo-order \
        --format="%H${RAD_RS}%ae${RAD_RS}%s" "$ref" 2>/dev/null)

    for emoji in ${survivor[@]+"${!survivor[@]}"}; do
        printf '%s%s%s%s%s\n' "${survivor[$emoji]%%"$RAD_US"*}" "$RAD_US" "$emoji" "$RAD_US" \
            "${survivor[$emoji]#*"$RAD_US"}"
    done | LC_ALL=C sort -t"$RAD_US" -k1,1n
    return 0
}

## <storage> <id> - the COB a reaction target names, in 'RAD_METADATA_REF'/_TYPE/_ID
function rad_reaction_ref
{
    ## A patch or issue id names the COB itself and the reader's listing says which; a *revision* id
    ## is an op inside a patch COB, and 'git for-each-ref --contains' finds the COB whose chain
    ## carries it -- the same resolution 'rad patch react' makes when it is handed a revision.
    ##
    ## Returns 3 when nothing matches, 2 when the id matches more than one COB.
    local storage="$1" id="$2" ref=""
    local rc=0
    rad_comment_metadata_ref "$storage" "$id"
    rc=$?
    if (( rc == 0 )); then
        RAD_METADATA_REF="$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_REF"
        RAD_METADATA_TYPE="$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_TYPE"
        RAD_METADATA_ID="$RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ID"
        return 0
    fi
    if (( rc == 2 )); then
        error "'$id' matches more than one issue or patch, use more characters"
        return 2
    fi
    ref="$(git --git-dir="$storage" for-each-ref --contains="$id" --format='%(refname)' \
        'refs/namespaces/*/refs/cobs/xyz.radicle.patch/*' 2>/dev/null | LC_ALL=C sort | head -1)" || ref=""
    if [[ -z "$ref" ]]; then
        error "the node holds no issue, patch or revision '$id' in this repository"
        return 3
    fi
    RAD_METADATA_REF="$ref"
    RAD_METADATA_TYPE="patch"
    RAD_METADATA_ID="${ref##*/}"
    return 0
}

## - the NID of the identity 'rad' uses here
function rad_metadata_self
{
    ## What is *ours*: the actor of an op travels in its author mail ('<alias>@<nid>'), and reactions
    ## are private per identity, so our NID is what decides which markers this caller wrote.
    local did=""
    did="$(rad_run self --did)" || return $?
    did="$(rad_metadata_nid "${did##*$'\n'}")"
    if [[ "$did" != z6M* ]]; then
        error "'$RAD_BIN self --did' names no radicle identity"
        return 1
    fi
    printf '%s' "$did"
}

function rad_reaction_usage ## [reason] - usage on stderr
{
    [[ -n "${1:-}" ]] && printf 'rad_reaction: %s\n' "$1" >&2
    printf 'usage: rad_reaction [--from <emoji>] [--repo <rid|path>] <to> <target>..\n' >&2
    printf '       rad_reaction <from> <to> <target>.. [--repo <rid|path>]\n' >&2
    printf '       <to>     the marker to set; empty withdraws <from> and sets nothing (patch only)\n' >&2
    printf '       <from>   the marker the caller expects there; omitted is no expectation at all,\n' >&2
    printf '                the empty string is "no live marker of mine"\n' >&2
    printf '       <target> <issue-id> (the issue itself), <issue-id> <comment-id>,\n' >&2
    printf '                <patch-or-revision-id> (the patch is its root revision),\n' >&2
    printf '                <patch-or-revision-id> <comment-id>\n' >&2
}

function rad_reaction ## see the module header
{
    ## Compare and swap on the caller's *own* reaction state: one target, one marker, and the write
    ## happens only when what the caller expects is what the store reduces.  rad has no way to set a
    ## reaction to a state, only to add one, so 'from -> to' is what a transition is.
    ##
    ## Patch level withdraws <from> in the same step ('--undo' exists there), so the target ends with
    ## one live marker.  Issue level cannot: 'rad issue react' has no '--undo' in rad 1.10.3, so the
    ## transition is supersede-only and the check is a guard, never a cleanup -- the older marker
    ## stays in the chain, and the newest live one is what a reader resolves.
    local -a args=() positional=() targets=() setcall=() dropcall=()
    local raw value from="" to="" repo="" kind="" level="" storage="" ref="" rid="" nid="" type=""
    local state="" live="" withdrawn="" target="" pos emoji active op
    local from_given=""
    local -i i=0 count=0

    for raw in "$@"; do
        if [[ "$raw" == --*=* ]]; then
            args+=("${raw%%=*}" "${raw#*=}")
        else
            args+=("$raw")
        fi
    done
    count=${#args[@]}
    while (( i < count )); do
        raw="${args[i]}"
        i=$(( i + 1 ))
        case "$raw" in
        -h | --help)
            rad_reaction_usage 2>&1
            return 0
            ;;
        --from | --repo)
            if (( i >= count )); then
                rad_reaction_usage "'$raw' needs a value"
                return 2
            fi
            value="${args[i]}"
            i=$(( i + 1 ))
            case "$raw" in
            --from)
                from="$value"
                from_given=1
                ;;
            --repo) repo="$value" ;;
            esac
            ;;
        -*)
            rad_reaction_usage "unknown argument '$raw'"
            return 2
            ;;
        *) positional+=( "$raw" ) ;;
        esac
    done

    if (( ${#positional[@]} == 0 )); then
        rad_reaction_usage "the reaction to set and its target are required"
        return 2
    fi
    ## The '<from> <to>' form is the two leading positionals, the '<to> <target>' form starts with
    ## the reaction to set.  What tells them apart is what can be a reaction at all: a target is an
    ## id, never an emoji -- and an empty second word is only a '<to>' if that is what it can be,
    ## which is how 'rad_reaction <from> "" <target>' takes a marker back.
    if (( ${#positional[@]} >= 3 )) && [[ -z "$from_given" ]] &&
        { rad_reaction_accepts "${positional[1]}" ||
            { [[ -z "${positional[1]}" ]] && rad_reaction_accepts "${positional[0]}"; }; }; then
        from="${positional[0]}"
        from_given=1
        to="${positional[1]}"
        targets=( "${positional[@]:2}" )
    else
        to="${positional[0]}"
        targets=( "${positional[@]:1}" )
    fi
    if (( ${#targets[@]} == 0 || ${#targets[@]} > 2 )); then
        rad_reaction_usage "one target (and its comment) is required, got ${#targets[@]} words"
        return 2
    fi
    if [[ -n "$to" ]] && ! rad_reaction_accepts "$to"; then
        rad_reaction_usage "'$to' is not a reaction the store accepts: one emoji, no sequence"
        return 2
    fi
    if [[ -z "$to" ]] && [[ -z "$from" || -z "$from_given" ]]; then
        rad_reaction_usage "an empty reaction only takes back the one the caller expects"
        return 2
    fi
    if [[ -n "$from_given" && "$from" == "$to" ]]; then
        rad_reaction_usage "'$from' to '$to' is not a transition"
        return 2
    fi

    is_command_installed "$RAD_BIN" || {
        error "'$RAD_BIN' is not installed"
        return 1
    }
    rad_metadata_store rad_reaction "$repo" || return $?
    storage="$RAD_METADATA_STORAGE"
    rid="$RAD_METADATA_RID"
    nid="$(rad_metadata_self)" || return $?

    ## Which COB the target names, and which of its reactions: the issue itself (its root comment),
    ## a comment of the issue, the patch (whose id is its root revision) or one revision of it, or a
    ## comment of a revision.
    rad_reaction_ref "$storage" "${targets[0]}" || return $?
    ref="$RAD_METADATA_REF"
    type="$RAD_METADATA_TYPE"
    if [[ "$type" == issue ]]; then
        kind="comment"
        if (( ${#targets[@]} == 1 )); then
            level="root"
            target="$RAD_METADATA_ID"
        else
            level="comment"
            target="${targets[1]}"
        fi
    else
        if (( ${#targets[@]} == 1 )); then
            kind="revision"
            level="revision"
            target="${targets[0]}"
        else
            kind="revision-comment"
            level="comment"
            target="${targets[1]}"
        fi
    fi

    ## Taking a marker back is a patch level act: 'rad issue react' has no '--undo'.  Asked before the
    ## state is even read -- no state would make it possible.
    if [[ -z "$to" && "$type" != patch ]]; then
        error "a reaction on an issue or one of its comments cannot be taken back: '$RAD_BIN issue react' has no --undo"
        return 1
    fi

    ## Our own state on that target, newest survivor last.
    state="$(rad_metadata_reactions "$storage" "$ref" "$kind" "$target" "$nid")"
    live=""
    while IFS="$RAD_US" read -r pos emoji active op; do
        [[ -n "$emoji" ]] || continue
        [[ "$active" == true ]] || continue
        live="$emoji"
    done <<<"$state"
    if [[ -n "$from_given" && "$live" != "$from" ]]; then
        local found="no marker" expected="no marker"
        [[ -n "$live" ]] && found="'$live'"
        [[ -n "$from" ]] && expected="'$from'"
        error "the state of $type:${target} is $found where $expected was expected: nothing was written"
        return 1
    fi

    ## The write hands rad the target the caller named (short or full, 'rad' resolves both); the COB
    ## was only resolved to read the state back.
    case "$level:$type" in
    root:issue) setcall=( issue react "${targets[0]}" --to "${targets[0]}" --emoji "$to" --quiet ) ;;
    comment:issue) setcall=( issue react "${targets[0]}" --to "${targets[1]}" --emoji "$to" --quiet ) ;;
    revision:patch) setcall=( patch react "${targets[0]}" --emoji "$to" --quiet ) ;;
    comment:patch) setcall=( patch comment "${targets[0]}" --react "${targets[1]}" --emoji "$to" --quiet ) ;;
    esac
    case "$level:$type" in
    revision:patch) dropcall=( patch react "${targets[0]}" --emoji "$from" --undo --quiet ) ;;
    comment:patch) dropcall=( patch comment "${targets[0]}" --react "${targets[1]}" --emoji "$from" --undo --quiet ) ;;
    *) dropcall=() ;;
    esac

    ## Set first, take the old one back after: an interrupted step leaves the newer marker live, which
    ## is the state a reader resolves -- the order the reaction loop already proved in production.
    if [[ -n "$to" ]]; then
        rad_metadata_rad "$repo" "${setcall[@]}" || return $?
    fi
    if (( ${#dropcall[@]} )) && [[ -n "$from" && "$from" == "$live" ]]; then
        rad_metadata_rad "$repo" "${dropcall[@]}" || return $?
        withdrawn=yes
    fi

    printf 'applied reaction item=%s:%s level=%s target=%s from=%s to=%s withdrawn=%s\n' \
        "$type" "$RAD_METADATA_ID" "$level" "$target" "${from:--}" "${to:--}" "${withdrawn:-no}"
}

function rad_topic_usage ## [reason] - usage on stderr
{
    [[ -n "${1:-}" ]] && printf 'rad_topic: %s\n' "$1" >&2
    printf 'usage: rad_topic <class> <description> [--root <clone>] [--base <ref>] [--no-fetch] [--dry-run] [--json]\n' >&2
}

function rad_topic_json ## <root> <name> <path> <base> <head> <fetched> - one object
{
    printf '{"bar_rad":1,"action":"topic","root":%s,"name":%s,"path":%s,"branch":%s,"base":%s,"head":%s,"fetched":%s}\n' \
        "$(json_string "$1")" \
        "$(json_string "$2")" \
        "$(json_string "$3")" \
        "$(json_string "$2")" \
        "$(json_string "$4")" \
        "$(json_string "$5")" \
        "$(json_bool "$6")"
}

function rad_topic ## see the module header
{
    ## Body of the 'rad_topic' rule.  Nothing is created before the class, the name, the clone,
    ## the base and the collisions have all been resolved, so a refusal leaves no half-made tree.
    local -a args=()
    local raw arg
    local json="" root="" base="" fetch="yes" dry="" class="" description=""
    local -a classes=()
    local value line known="" slug name name_len wanted
    local tree main_tree remote="" branch="" fetched="none" behind="" head out err

    for raw in "$@"; do
        if [[ "$raw" == --*=* ]]; then
            args+=("${raw%%=*}" "${raw#*=}")
        else
            args+=("$raw")
        fi
    done
    local -i i=0 count=${#args[@]}
    while (( i < count )); do
        arg="${args[i]}"
        i=$(( i + 1 ))
        case "$arg" in
        -h | --help)
            rad_topic_usage 2>&1
            return 0
            ;;
        --json) json=1 ;;
        --dry-run) dry=1 ;;
        --no-fetch) fetch="" ;;
        --root | --base)
            if (( i >= count )) || [[ -z "${args[i]}" ]]; then
                rad_topic_usage "'$arg' needs a non-empty value"
                return 2
            fi
            value="${args[i]}"
            i=$(( i + 1 ))
            case "$arg" in
            --root) root="$value" ;;
            --base) base="$value" ;;
            esac
            ;;
        -*)
            rad_topic_usage "unknown argument '$arg'"
            return 2
            ;;
        *)
            if [[ -z "$class" ]]; then
                class="$arg"
            elif [[ -z "$description" ]]; then
                description="$arg"
            else
                rad_topic_usage "unexpected argument '$arg'"
                return 2
            fi
            ;;
        esac
    done

    [[ -n "${BAR_AGENT_JSON:-}" ]] && json=1
    if [[ -z "$class" || -z "$description" ]]; then
        rad_topic_usage "a class and a description are required"
        return 2
    fi

    while IFS= read -r line; do
        classes+=("$line")
    done < <(rad_classes)
    if (( ${#classes[@]} == 0 )); then
        printf 'rad_topic: cannot read the branch classes from %s\n' "${BAR_DIR:-?}/git" >&2
        return 2
    fi
    wanted="${class%-}-"
    for line in "${classes[@]}"; do
        [[ "$line" == "$wanted" ]] && known=yes
    done
    if [[ -z "$known" ]]; then
        rad_topic_usage "unknown class '$class', want one of: ${classes[*]}"
        return 2
    fi

    slug="$(rad_slug "$description")"
    if [[ -z "$slug" ]]; then
        rad_topic_usage "the description '$description' has no usable character"
        return 2
    fi
    name="${wanted}${slug}"
    name_len=${#name}
    if (( name_len > 40 )); then
        printf 'rad_topic: the name is %d characters, at most 40 are allowed: %s -- shorten the description\n' \
            "$name_len" "$name" >&2
        return 1
    fi

    ## The clone: always the *main* checkout, also when the caller sits inside a topic tree.
    if [[ -n "$root" ]]; then
        if ! tree="$(git -C "$root" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)"; then
            rad_topic_usage "'$root' is not a git repository"
            return 2
        fi
    elif ! tree="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)"; then
        rad_topic_usage "not inside a git repository, pass --root <clone>"
        return 2
    fi
    main_tree="$(git -C "$tree" worktree list --porcelain 2>/dev/null |
        LC_ALL=C awk '/^worktree / { print $2; exit }')"
    [[ -n "$main_tree" ]] || main_tree="$tree"

    ## The base: the clone's trunk -- upstream *plus* the local work, so branching off the remote
    ## tip would drop everything this host has not published yet.  The remote is fetched first to
    ## *know* the relation; a trunk which is behind upstream is refused, because updating the trunk
    ## is its own step with its own gate.
    if [[ -n "$base" ]]; then
        :
    else
        branch="$(git -C "$main_tree" symbolic-ref --quiet --short HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)"
        if git -C "$main_tree" remote get-url rad >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            remote="rad"
        elif git -C "$main_tree" remote get-url origin >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            remote="origin"
        fi
        if [[ -n "$remote" && -n "$fetch" ]]; then
            if git -C "$main_tree" fetch --quiet --no-tags "$remote" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                fetched="yes"
            else
                fetched="no"
                printf 'rad_topic: could not fetch %s, the relation to the trunk is stale\n' "$remote" >&2
            fi
        fi
        if [[ "$fetched" == "yes" && -n "$remote" && -n "$branch" ]] &&
            git -C "$main_tree" rev-parse --verify --quiet "refs/remotes/$remote/$branch" >/dev/null; then
            behind="$(git -C "$main_tree" rev-list --count "refs/heads/$branch..refs/remotes/$remote/$branch" 2>/dev/null || true)"
            if [[ -n "$behind" && "$behind" != "0" ]]; then
                printf 'rad_topic: the trunk %s is %s commit(s) behind %s/%s -- update the trunk first (fetch, merge upstream into it, run its gate) or name the base with --base\n' \
                    "$branch" "$behind" "$remote" "$branch" >&2
                return 1
            fi
        fi
        if [[ -n "$branch" ]]; then
            base="$branch"
        else
            base="HEAD"
        fi
    fi

    local wt_dir="$main_tree/.worktrees"
    local wt_path="$wt_dir/$name"
    if [[ -e "$wt_path" ]]; then
        printf 'rad_topic: the tree already exists: %s\n' "$wt_path" >&2
        return 1
    fi
    if git -C "$main_tree" show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$name"; then
        printf 'rad_topic: the branch already exists: %s\n' "$name" >&2
        return 1
    fi

    if [[ -n "$dry" ]]; then
        if [[ -n "$json" ]]; then
            rad_topic_json "$main_tree" "$name" "$wt_path" "$base" "-" "$fetched"
        else
            printf '%s  %s  base=%s\n' "$wt_path" "$name" "$base"
        fi
        return 0
    fi

    rad_exclude_worktrees "$main_tree"
    mkdir -p "$wt_dir" 2>/dev/null || {
        printf 'rad_topic: cannot create %s\n' "$wt_dir" >&2
        return 2
    }
    if ! err="$(git -C "$main_tree" worktree add --quiet "$wt_path" -b "$name" "$base" 2>&1)"; then
        printf 'rad_topic: git worktree add failed: %s\n' "${err:-unknown error}" >&2
        return 2
    fi
    head="$(git -C "$wt_path" rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null || printf '-')"

    if [[ -n "$json" ]]; then
        rad_topic_json "$main_tree" "$name" "$wt_path" "$base" "$head" "$fetched"
    else
        out="$(printf '%s  %s  base=%s  head=%s' "$wt_path" "$name" "$base" "$head")"
        printf '%s\n' "$out"
    fi
    return 0
}
